### de b01
001 the Military coup => der Militärputsch | alts=der Staatsstreich des Militärs | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Der Militärputsch
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political vocabulary for an unlawful seizure of state power carried out by the armed forces.
TRAP: Putsch alone need not be military; Militärputsch fixes the agent, whereas Staatsstreich is broader and may be carried out by civilian elites.
EX: Der Militärputsch beendete den kurzen demokratischen Übergang und verschaffte den Streitkräften dauerhaften Einfluss auf Justiz, Medien und Wirtschaftsordnung. || The military coup ended the brief democratic transition and gave the armed forces lasting influence over the judiciary, media and economic order.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the alternative records the broader paraphrase without treating every Staatsstreich as a military coup.
006 the Guerillas => die Guerillakämpfer | alts=die Guerillas | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Guerillakämpfer
REG: Neutral historical and security-policy term for people engaged in irregular armed struggle; Guerillas is common but less explicit.
TRAP: Die Guerilla usually denotes guerrilla warfare or a guerrilla organization collectively, not one individual fighter; Guerillakämpfer makes the human plural unambiguous.
EX: Nach dem Waffenstillstand weigerten sich viele Guerillakämpfer, ihre Waffen abzugeben, weil weder Schutzprogramme finanziert noch die zugesagten Sicherheitsgarantien international überwacht wurden. || After the ceasefire, many guerrillas refused to surrender their weapons because protection programs lacked funding and the promised security guarantees had no international monitoring.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back: Guerillas is valid, but Guerillakämpfer more reliably denotes people rather than the movement or method of warfare. Self-review separated funding for protection programs from monitoring of security guarantees.
011 the Capitalism => der Kapitalismus | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Kapitalismus
REG: Neutral academic and political term; evaluative force comes from context, and general statements commonly omit the article.
TRAP: Spanish, Portuguese, Italian and French often use an article with abstract ideological nouns where German naturally uses zero article, as in Kapitalismus wurde gleichgesetzt.
EX: Im westlichen Bündnis wurde Kapitalismus häufig mit politischer Freiheit gleichgesetzt, obwohl autoritäre Partnerstaaten zentrale Wirtschaftssektoren weiterhin eng kontrollierten. || In the Western alliance, capitalism was often equated with political freedom, although authoritarian partner states continued to control key economic sectors closely.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
012 the Torture => die Folter | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Folter
REG: Neutral-formal legal, historical and human-rights vocabulary for the deliberate infliction of severe physical or psychological suffering.
TRAP: Tortur exists in German but often denotes an ordeal or an especially painful procedure; Folter is the unambiguous institutional and legal term for torture.
EX: Die Geheimdienste setzten Folter systematisch ein, um Oppositionelle einzuschüchtern, Geständnisse zu erzwingen und Informationen über verdeckte Netzwerke zu gewinnen. || The intelligence services systematically used torture to intimidate opposition figures, force confessions and obtain information about covert networks.
NOTE: —
013 the Dissidents => die Dissidenten | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Dissidenten
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political plural for people who publicly oppose the doctrine or rule of an authoritarian system.
TRAP: Dissident is a weak masculine noun: the singular accusative and dative are den/dem Dissidenten, the same visible ending used in the plural headword.
EX: Viele Dissidenten veröffentlichten ihre Texte im Ausland, weil staatliche Verlage kritische Analysen zensierten und unabhängige Zeitschriften keine Druckgenehmigung erhielten. || Many dissidents published their texts abroad because state publishers censored critical analysis and independent journals received no printing permits.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; the card adds the weak-noun morphology absent from the isolated gloss. Self-review replaced a vague claim that journals could scarcely appear with the concrete denial of printing permits.
014 the Regime => das Regime | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Das Regime
REG: Neutral-formal in analytical description, though it often carries a critical implication when used for an authoritarian government.
TRAP: Unlike masculine French le régime and Italian il regime, German Regime is neuter; its nominative plural is die Regime, not *die Regimen.
EX: Das Regime sicherte seine Herrschaft durch Patronagenetze, Notverordnungen und eine gelenkte Presse, die politische Gewalt als notwendige Stabilisierung darstellte. || The regime secured its rule through patronage networks, emergency decrees and a controlled press that portrayed political violence as necessary stabilization.
NOTE: —
015 the Soviet influence => der sowjetische Einfluss | alts=der Einfluss der Sowjetunion | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Der sowjetische Einfluss
REG: Neutral historical and geopolitical wording; the genitive alternative is useful when the institutional actor needs stronger emphasis.
TRAP: German writes the relational adjective sowjetisch with a lowercase initial; after der it takes weak -e, but without an article it becomes sowjetischer Einfluss.
EX: Der sowjetische Einfluss auf die Partei wuchs in den folgenden Jahren, nachdem Moskau Kredite, Militärberater und vorteilhafte Handelsabkommen angeboten hatte. || Soviet influence over the party grew in the following years after Moscow had offered loans, military advisers and favorable trade agreements.
NOTE: Self-review replaced bevorzugte Handelsabkommen, which could mean favored agreements, with vorteilhafte Handelsabkommen to express favorable terms unambiguously.
016 Authoritarian => autoritär | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=autoritär
REG: Neutral-formal political adjective for rule that demands obedience and sharply limits pluralism or institutional accountability.
TRAP: English authoritarian and the Romance forms autoritario/autoritaire do not transfer their ending into German; the adjective is autoritär and inflects normally.
EX: Die Regierung blieb autoritär, obwohl sie Wahlen abhielt, denn Oppositionelle hatten keinen fairen Medienzugang und unabhängige Gerichte wurden systematisch geschwächt. || The government remained authoritarian despite holding elections, because opposition figures lacked fair media access and independent courts were systematically weakened.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report. The lowercase headword also corrects the legacy capitalization of an isolated adjective.
018 the Exile => das Exil | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Im Exil
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political noun for forced or prolonged residence outside one's country.
TRAP: The conventional location is im Exil and movement is ins Exil; English and Romance learners should not transfer a bare preposition without German case marking.
EX: Im Exil gründeten ehemalige Minister eine Zeitschrift, die geheime Abkommen dokumentierte und internationale Unterstützung für demokratische Reformen mobilisierte. || In exile, former ministers founded a journal that documented secret agreements and mobilized international support for democratic reforms.
NOTE: The example deliberately tests the high-value fixed locative im Exil rather than merely repeating the citation form.
019 the Nationalization => die Verstaatlichung | alts=die Überführung in Staatseigentum | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Verstaatlichung
REG: Neutral-formal economic and public-policy term for transferring a private enterprise or asset into state ownership.
TRAP: English nationalization and Romance cognates may suggest Nationalisierung; that form is possible, but Verstaatlichung unambiguously expresses transfer into state ownership.
EX: Die Verstaatlichung der Kupferminen sollte öffentliche Einnahmen sichern, löste jedoch Entschädigungsforderungen aus und verschärfte den Konflikt mit ausländischen Investoren. || The nationalization of the copper mines was intended to secure public revenue, but it triggered compensation claims and intensified the conflict with foreign investors.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is the preferred idiomatic term; the descriptive alternative preserves the ownership-transfer sense without the ambiguity of Nationalisierung.
020 the Junta => die Junta | alts=die Militärjunta | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Junta
REG: Neutral historical and political loanword, normally referring in German to a military council or the government it controls.
TRAP: German Junta is feminine and has the plural Juntas; Portuguese junta can denote an ordinary board, but the unqualified German loanword is politically narrower.
EX: Die Junta versprach eine rasche Rückkehr zur Zivilregierung, verlängerte aber den Ausnahmezustand und verwies immer weitere politische Verfahren an Militärgerichte. || The junta promised a rapid return to civilian government, but extended the state of emergency and assigned ever more political cases to military courts.
NOTE: Self-review replaced the grammatical but bureaucratically compressed Verfahren übertragen with the more idiomatic Verfahren an Militärgerichte verweisen.
021 the Counterinsurgency => die Aufstandsbekämpfung | alts=die Bekämpfung von Aufständen | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Aufstandsbekämpfung
REG: Formal military, security-policy and historical term covering coordinated measures against an organized insurgency.
TRAP: Do not mechanically reproduce English counter- as *Gegeninsurgenz; Aufstandsbekämpfung is the established transparent German term and includes the linking -s-.
EX: Die Aufstandsbekämpfung verband militärische Operationen mit Geheimdienstarbeit, psychologischer Kriegführung und Entwicklungsprogrammen, deren ziviler Nutzen den Sicherheitszielen meist untergeordnet blieb. || The counterinsurgency combined military operations with intelligence work, psychological warfare and development programs whose civilian benefits generally remained subordinate to security objectives.
NOTE: Self-review replaced the strained comparison Wirkung hinter Zielen with the idiomatic relation Nutzen den Sicherheitszielen unterordnen.
022 the OAS (Organization of American States) => die Organisation Amerikanischer Staaten (OAS) | alts=die OAS | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Organisation Amerikanischer Staaten (OAS)
REG: Formal institutional name; spell out the organization at first mention and use the feminine abbreviation OAS thereafter.
TRAP: In the official proper name, Amerikanischer is capitalized and there is no article before Staaten; *Organisation der Amerikanischen Staaten is not the conventional name.
EX: Die Organisation Amerikanischer Staaten (OAS) entsandte Wahlbeobachter, vermied jedoch eine klare Verurteilung der Regierung, um den regionalen Konsens nicht zu gefährden. || The Organization of American States (OAS) sent election observers but avoided clearly condemning the government so as not to jeopardize regional consensus.
NOTE: The headword reverses the legacy display order so the full institutional name introduces its abbreviation.
023 the Proxy wars => die Stellvertreterkriege | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Stellvertreterkriegen
REG: Neutral-formal historical and geopolitical plural for wars in which outside powers pursue rivalry through locally armed or supported parties.
TRAP: Proxy is not normally retained as an English modifier here; the established German compound is Stellvertreterkrieg, with plural -kriege and no hyphen.
EX: In mehreren Stellvertreterkriegen lieferten die Supermächte Waffen und Berater, während lokale Akteure die politischen und menschlichen Kosten trugen. || In several proxy wars, the superpowers supplied weapons and advisers while local actors bore the political and human costs.
NOTE: —
025 the Repression => die Repression | alts=die staatliche Unterdrückung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Repression
REG: Formal political and social-science term; singular Repression denotes a system or practice, while Repressionen often denotes individual coercive measures.
TRAP: Do not assume singular and plural are freely interchangeable: staatliche Repression is systemic, whereas staatliche Repressionen usually means separate punitive measures.
EX: Staatliche Repression beschränkte sich nicht auf Verhaftungen, sondern umfasste Berufsverbote, Reisebeschränkungen und die systematische Überwachung oppositioneller Milieus im ganzen Land. || State repression was not limited to arrests but included professional bans, travel restrictions and the systematic surveillance of opposition milieus throughout the country.
NOTE: Materially diverges from old_back: Unterdrückung remains a valid broad alternative, but Repression is the sharper analytical headword for organized coercive measures.
026 the Diplomacy => die Diplomatie | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=die Diplomatie
REG: Neutral-formal term for the practice and processes of managing international relations through negotiation and representation.
TRAP: Diplomatie denotes the practice or institutional process, not the people conducting it; for the latter use Diplomaten or der diplomatische Dienst.
EX: Hinter den öffentlichen Drohungen schuf die Diplomatie vertrauliche Kanäle, über die beide Seiten Gefangenenaustausche und erste Rüstungskontrollabkommen schrittweise aushandelten. || Behind the public threats, diplomacy created confidential channels through which both sides gradually negotiated prisoner exchanges and initial arms-control agreements.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB. Self-review replaced abstract Rüstungskontrollen with negotiable Rüstungskontrollabkommen.
028 the Embassies => die Botschaften | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Botschaften
REG: Neutral institutional plural for official diplomatic missions headed by ambassadors; context distinguishes it from the identical plural meaning messages.
TRAP: Botschaft means both embassy and message; Romance cognates such as embajada, embaixada, ambassade and ambasciata do not yield a German cognate.
EX: Mehrere Botschaften gewährten verfolgten Gewerkschaftern vorübergehend Schutz, während ihre Regierungen anschließend diskret über sichere Ausreisen in aufnahmebereite Drittstaaten verhandelten. || Several embassies temporarily sheltered persecuted trade unionists while their governments subsequently negotiated discreetly over safe departures to third countries willing to admit them.
NOTE: Self-review replaced the vague politische Aufnahmeprogramme with the concrete and historically plausible destination aufnahmebereite Drittstaaten.
029 the Trade barriers => die Handelshemmnisse | alts=die Handelsschranken; die Handelsbarrieren | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Handelshemmnisse
REG: Formal trade-policy term covering tariff and non-tariff measures that obstruct cross-border exchange; the alternatives are less terminologically precise but common.
TRAP: The compound takes a linking -s- and the plural of Hemmnis is Hemmnisse: Handelshemmnisse, not *Handelhemmnisse or *Handelshemmnise.
EX: Die neuen Handelshemmnisse verteuerten Maschinenimporte, behinderten den Technologietransfer und lieferten der Regierung zugleich einen Vorwand für weitere gezielte Marktinterventionen. || The new trade barriers made machinery imports more expensive, hindered technology transfer and simultaneously gave the government a pretext for further targeted market interventions.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back by selecting Handelshemmnisse, the conventional umbrella term in trade policy; Handelsschranken remains a valid alternative.
030 the Censorship => die Zensur | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Zensur
REG: Neutral-formal media, political and historical term for institutional control or suppression of publication and expression.
TRAP: English censorship corresponds to the institutional headword Zensur; the rare process noun Zensurierung, where used, foregrounds the act rather than the system.
EX: Die Zensur erfasste nicht nur Zeitungen, sondern auch wissenschaftliche Zeitschriften, deren historische Beiträge angeblich das nationale Ansehen beschädigten. || Censorship affected not only newspapers but also academic journals whose historical articles allegedly damaged the country's reputation.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
031 the Refugees => die Flüchtlinge | alts=die Geflüchteten | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Flüchtlinge
REG: Flüchtlinge is the neutral established legal and historical plural; Geflüchtete is common in current institutional and inclusive usage but not always legally coextensive.
TRAP: Geflüchtete declines as a substantivized adjective, whereas Flüchtling is a noun; do not transfer the endings mechanically between die Geflüchteten and die Flüchtlinge.
EX: Viele Flüchtlinge blieben jahrelang in Nachbarstaaten, weil die fortdauernde politische Verfolgung eine sichere Rückkehr trotz formeller Amnestien unmöglich machte. || Many refugees remained in neighboring states for years because continuing political persecution made a safe return impossible despite formal amnesties.
NOTE: The alternative is usage-sensitive: it is natural for people who have fled, but legal texts may require the narrower status term Flüchtlinge.
032 the Neoliberalism => der Neoliberalismus | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Der Neoliberalismus
REG: Neutral-formal term in political economy and intellectual history; it can be descriptive or critical depending on the analytical context.
TRAP: Neoliberalismus names a doctrine or policy tradition; neoliberal is the inflected adjective and cannot stand in for the noun as it can in some compressed English prose.
EX: Der Neoliberalismus gewann in den achtziger Jahren an Einfluss, als internationale Kreditgeber Privatisierungen, Haushaltsdisziplin und Handelsöffnung zu Voraussetzungen für neue Darlehen machten. || Neoliberalism gained influence in the 1980s as international lenders made privatization, fiscal discipline and trade liberalization conditions for new loans.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB. Self-review replaced the abstract Reformagenda claim with the historically concrete conditionality of new loans.
034 the Cold War alliances => die Bündnisse des Kalten Krieges | alts=die Allianzen des Kalten Krieges | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Bündnisse des Kalten Krieges
REG: Neutral-formal historical plural; Bündnisse is the idiomatic default, while Allianzen is valid and can sound more strategic or institutional.
TRAP: The historical name is der Kalte Krieg; after des both the substantivized adjective and noun remain capitalized: des Kalten Krieges.
EX: Die Bündnisse des Kalten Krieges prägten nicht nur die Verteidigungsplanung, sondern auch Handel, Technologietransfer und diplomatische Loyalitäten ihrer Mitgliedstaaten. || The Cold War alliances shaped not only defense planning but also trade, technology transfer and the diplomatic loyalties of their member states.
NOTE: Materially improves the legacy preference: Allianzen is possible, but Bündnisse is the less marked German headword in historical prose. Self-review replaced the abstract verb strukturierten with the more idiomatic prägten and supplied the article before Verteidigungsplanung.
035 the Containment => die Eindämmungspolitik | alts=die Politik der Eindämmung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Eindämmungspolitik
REG: Formal Cold War historiography for the strategy of limiting the further expansion of Soviet power or communism.
TRAP: Bare Eindämmung can describe containing anything from inflation to an epidemic; in Cold War history, Eindämmungspolitik identifies the strategic doctrine.
EX: Die Eindämmungspolitik zielte darauf, die sowjetische Expansion zu begrenzen, ohne einen direkten Krieg zwischen den Atommächten zu provozieren. || The policy of containment sought to limit Soviet expansion without provoking a direct war between the nuclear powers.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back: die Eindämmung is a correct literal rendering, but it does not by itself name the Cold War doctrine selected by the topic.
037 the Pro-U.S. factions => die proamerikanischen Gruppierungen | alts=die US-freundlichen Gruppierungen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=proamerikanische Gruppierungen
REG: Neutral-formal historical wording for organized political camps aligned with the United States; Gruppierungen avoids a specifically parliamentary reading.
TRAP: German normally writes proamerikanisch as one lowercase adjective; Fraktion often means a parliamentary caucus, so a literal Fraktionen may narrow the source incorrectly.
EX: Innerhalb der Koalition erhielten proamerikanische Gruppierungen finanzielle Unterstützung, während neutralistische Kräfte aus Ministerien und staatlichen Medien verdrängt wurden. || Within the coalition, pro-U.S. factions received financial support while neutralist forces were pushed out of ministries and state media.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back by avoiding Fraktionen, which can misleadingly imply formal parliamentary groups in an underspecified historical context.
039 Disappeared => die Verschwundenen | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Die Verschwundenen
REG: Formal historical and human-rights plural for people secretly abducted and whose fate state authorities conceal.
TRAP: The historical noun is a substantivized adjective and therefore capitalized and declined: die Verschwundenen, not the bare participle verschwunden supplied by the legacy gloss.
EX: Die Verschwundenen wurden in amtlichen Registern nicht geführt, während ihre Familien bei Behörden, in Archiven und vor Kasernen nach belastbaren Spuren suchten. || The disappeared were not listed in official registers while their families searched for reliable traces at government offices, in archives and outside barracks.
NOTE: The isolated English prompt is ambiguous. The Cold War and human-rights context supports the nominal sense the disappeared, which materially corrects adjectival old_back. Self-review replaced unerwähnt with the idiomatic administrative collocation in Registern nicht geführt.

### de b02
044 the Ideology => die Ideologie | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Ideologie
REG: Neutral-formal academic and political vocabulary for a coherent system of ideas; in critical prose it often implies distortion or doctrinal rigidity.
TRAP: The related adjective is ideologisch, not *ideologistisch; the noun's final -ie is also stressed in German: Ideologie.
EX: Die herrschende Ideologie erklärte soziale Konflikte zu ausländisch gesteuerten Störungen und entzog damit Forderungen nach Umverteilung jede politische Legitimität. || The ruling ideology portrayed social conflicts as foreign-controlled disturbances and thereby denied demands for redistribution any political legitimacy.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
045 the Arms race => das Wettrüsten | alts=der Rüstungswettlauf | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Wettrüsten
REG: Neutral historical and political vocabulary; Rüstungswettlauf is a slightly more analytical alternative that likewise foregrounds reciprocal military competition.
TRAP: The established compound is neuter Wettrüsten; *Waffenrennen is an English calque, and the alternative Rüstungswettlauf is masculine.
EX: Das nukleare Wettrüsten band enorme Haushaltsmittel, obwohl beide Supermächte wussten, dass zusätzliche Sprengköpfe ihre tatsächliche Sicherheit kaum erhöhten. || The nuclear arms race tied up enormous budgetary resources, although both superpowers knew that additional warheads scarcely increased their actual security.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the established analytical alternative and rejects the tempting literal calque.
046 Interventionism => der Interventionismus | alts=die interventionistische Politik | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Interventionismus
REG: Formal political-science term, often critical, for a doctrine or recurring practice of intervening in another state's affairs.
TRAP: Interventionismus denotes a doctrine or sustained tendency, not a single Intervention; its adjective is interventionistisch.
EX: Der amerikanische Interventionismus in Lateinamerika rechtfertigte verdeckte Operationen mit dem Schutz regionaler Stabilität, untergrub jedoch langfristig die Glaubwürdigkeit demokratischer Bündnisversprechen. || American interventionism in Latin America justified covert operations by invoking the protection of regional stability, but in the long term undermined the credibility of democratic alliance pledges.
NOTE: The isolated English can also refer to interventionist economic policy; the Cold War topic supports the foreign-policy sense used here. Self-review replaced als Schutz with the idiomatic justification frame mit dem Schutz.
047 the Coup d'état => der Staatsstreich | alts=der Putsch | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Staatsstreich
REG: Staatsstreich is the neutral-formal political and historical term; Putsch is common but can sound more journalistic and foreground force or conspiracy.
TRAP: Staatsstreich does not specify a military perpetrator; Militärputsch is narrower, so the two terms must not be treated as automatic equivalents.
EX: Der Staatsstreich stürzte nicht nur den Präsidenten, sondern löste auch die gewählten Regionalparlamente auf und setzte zentrale Grundrechte außer Kraft. || The coup d'état not only removed the president but also dissolved the elected regional parliaments and suspended key fundamental rights.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. This card preserves the useful distinction from Militärputsch, the explicitly military term retained in the preceding batch. Self-review changed beseitigte to stürzte to avoid a possible implication that the president was killed.
051 the Christian Democracy => die Christdemokratie | alts=die christdemokratische Bewegung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Christdemokratie
REG: Neutral political and historical term for the Christian-democratic current or movement; a specific party should normally be identified by its official name.
TRAP: Christdemokratie names the political current, whereas christliche Demokratie can merely describe democracy informed by Christian values and is not automatically a party name.
EX: Die Christdemokratie unterstützte zunächst institutionelle Reformen, wandte sich später aber gegen die Regierung, als Konflikte um Eigentum und Verfassung eskalierten. || Christian Democracy initially supported institutional reforms but later turned against the government as conflicts over property and the constitution escalated.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate for the movement. The card avoids silently equating that movement with any one national Christian-democratic party.
052 the Land redistribution => die Umverteilung von Land | alts=die Landumverteilung; die Neuverteilung von Land | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Umverteilung von Land
REG: Neutral-formal agrarian-policy wording for changing the ownership or control of agricultural land; the compact compound is common in analytical prose.
TRAP: Von Land means of land as property; vom Land contracts von dem Land and usually means from the countryside or from the land.
EX: Die Umverteilung von Land scheiterte vielerorts, weil Großgrundbesitzer gerichtliche Verfahren verzögerten und lokale Behörden die neuen Eigentumstitel nicht anerkannten. || Land redistribution failed in many places because large landowners delayed court proceedings and local authorities did not recognize the new property titles.
NOTE: The legacy phrase is accurate. Self-review kept this distinct from Agrarreform: redistribution changes landholding, while an agrarian reform may cover much more.
054 the Expropriation => die Enteignung | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Enteignung
REG: Neutral-formal legal and economic term for compulsory withdrawal of property rights by public authority; compensation is a separate legal question.
TRAP: Enteignung is not the same as Beschlagnahme, which is generally a seizure rather than a final transfer of ownership, or Verstaatlichung, which names state ownership.
EX: Die Enteignung ausländischer Bergbauunternehmen verschärfte den diplomatischen Konflikt, zumal die Regierungen monatelang über Bewertungsverfahren und angemessene Entschädigungen stritten. || The expropriation of foreign mining companies intensified the diplomatic conflict, especially as the governments argued for months over valuation procedures and adequate compensation.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card supplies the legal distinctions absent from the isolated gloss.
057 the Peasants => die Bauern | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Bauern
REG: Neutral in historical and social-class analysis; in current agricultural policy, Landwirte may be preferred when occupation rather than class position is intended.
TRAP: Bauer is a weak masculine noun: the singular accusative and dative are den/dem Bauern, identical in form to the nominative plural Bauern.
EX: Viele Bauern schlossen sich den Protesten an, nachdem steigende Pachtkosten und fehlender Zugang zu Krediten ihre wirtschaftliche Existenz unmittelbar bedroht hatten. || Many peasants joined the protests after rising lease costs and a lack of access to credit had directly threatened their economic survival.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; Bauern is retained over Landwirte because the Cold War context invokes a rural social class, not merely an occupation. Self-review replaced fehlende Kredite with the more precise fehlender Zugang zu Krediten.
061 the Militancy => die Militanz | alts=die kämpferische Haltung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Militanz
REG: Formal political and sociological term for a confrontational readiness to act; context determines whether actual violence is implied.
TRAP: Spanish militancia, Portuguese militância and Italian militanza can denote ordinary activism or membership; German Militanz usually implies a more confrontational stance.
EX: Die wachsende Militanz einzelner Jugendverbände zeigte sich zunehmend in Straßenkämpfen, bewaffneten Selbstschutzgruppen und der offenen Ablehnung parlamentarischer Kompromisse. || The growing militancy of individual youth organizations increasingly manifested itself in street fighting, armed self-defense groups and the open rejection of parliamentary compromises.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is formally accurate, but its Romance lookalikes make the stronger German connotation worth teaching explicitly.
063 the Agrarian reform => die Agrarreform | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Agrarreform
REG: Formal historical, agricultural and development-policy term for restructuring land tenure, production institutions or rural economic relations.
TRAP: Agrarreform is broader than Bodenreform: the latter focuses on land ownership or tenure, whereas an Agrarreform may also change credit, cooperatives and labor rights.
EX: Die Agrarreform umfasste neben der Neuverteilung großer Güter auch staatliche Beratungsprogramme, Genossenschaftskredite und neue Rechte für landlose Pächter. || In addition to redistributing large estates, the agrarian reform included government advisory programs, cooperative loans and new rights for landless tenants.
NOTE: Retained as the best member of the near-duplicate family; the earlier plural prompt Land reforms is dropped. Self-review separated this broad reform from land redistribution.
064 the Urbanization => die Verstädterung | alts=die Urbanisierung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Verstädterung
REG: Formal social-geography term for the quantitative growth of cities or the urban population; Urbanisierung can also cover the spread of urban ways of life.
TRAP: In careful geography, Verstädterung foregrounds demographic or spatial concentration, while Urbanisierung may denote the broader diffusion of urban forms and lifestyles.
EX: Die rasche Verstädterung überforderte kommunale Verwaltungen, während informelle Siedlungen ohne verlässliche Wasserversorgung, Schulen oder Anbindung an den öffentlichen Nahverkehr massenhaft entstanden. || Rapid urbanization overwhelmed municipal administrations while informal settlements sprang up on a massive scale without reliable water supplies, schools or links to public transport.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back by choosing Verstädterung for the demographic process fixed by the example; the unqualified English also permits broader Urbanisierung. Self-review replaced öffentliche Verkehrsanbindungen with the idiomatic Anbindung an den öffentlichen Nahverkehr.
066 the Trade unions => die Gewerkschaften | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Gewerkschaften
REG: Neutral institutional plural for organizations that represent employees collectively in labor relations and public policy.
TRAP: A trade union is a Gewerkschaft, not a German Union; Romance sindicato or syndicat must not be transferred as Syndikat, which has different meanings.
EX: Unabhängige Gewerkschaften organisierten landesweit branchenübergreifende Streiks, obwohl Sicherheitsbehörden ihre Konten sperrten, Versammlungen auflösten und führende Funktionäre wiederholt festnahmen. || Independent trade unions organized cross-sector strikes nationwide, although security authorities froze their accounts, dissolved meetings and repeatedly arrested leading officials.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the noncognate headword and the Syndikat false friend give the item clear production value.
068 the Redistribution => die Umverteilung | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Umverteilung
REG: Neutral-formal political-economy term, normally specified by what is redistributed, such as Einkommen, Vermögen or Ressourcen.
TRAP: Neuverteilung suggests assigning something anew and is not an automatic substitute in fiscal policy; the standard term there is Umverteilung, often followed by von plus dative.
EX: Die progressive Steuerreform bewirkte nur begrenzte Umverteilung, da hohe Kapitaleinkommen weiterhin begünstigt und öffentliche Transferleistungen zugleich gekürzt wurden. || The progressive tax reform produced only limited redistribution because high capital income continued to receive favorable treatment while public transfer payments were cut.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. This general fiscal concept remains distinct from the specifically agrarian Umverteilung von Land retained earlier.
069 the Inequality => die Ungleichheit | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Ungleichheit
REG: Neutral academic and policy term for structural disparity; the plural Ungleichheiten is common when several dimensions or forms are distinguished.
TRAP: Ungleichheit denotes disparity, whereas Ungleichbehandlung denotes unequal treatment by an actor or institution; the two are not interchangeable.
EX: Soziale Ungleichheit nahm trotz steigender Durchschnittseinkommen zu, weil Vermögensgewinne bei wenigen Familien konzentriert blieben und ländliche Regionen von öffentlichen Investitionen kaum profitierten. || Social inequality increased despite rising average incomes because wealth gains remained concentrated among a few families and rural regions barely benefited from public investment.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card records the analytically important contrast with unequal treatment.
070 Left-wing => linksgerichtet | alts=linksorientiert | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=Linksgerichtete
REG: Neutral-formal political adjective; simple links is more compact in predicative use, while linksgerichtet is especially clear before a noun.
TRAP: The English prompt is adjectival, but der linke Flügel means the left wing as a noun phrase; attributive German requires an inflected form such as linke or linksgerichtete.
EX: Linksgerichtete Parteien forderten eine stärkere Umverteilung, unterschieden sich jedoch deutlich in ihrer Haltung zu Bündnisfreiheit, Verstaatlichung und bewaffnetem Widerstand. || Left-wing parties demanded greater redistribution but differed markedly in their stance toward nonalignment, nationalization and armed resistance.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which changes an adjective into the noun phrase der linke Flügel. The example supplies the required inflection and sentence-initial capitalization.
072 Centrism => der Zentrismus | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Zentrismus
REG: Formal political-science term for a position or strategy oriented toward the political center; it may be descriptive or critically assigned by opponents.
TRAP: Romance centrismo maps to German Zentrismus, not *Zentrismo; Zentrum means center and can also evoke a historical party, but it does not mean centrism.
EX: Der Zentrismus der Parteiführung beruhigte städtische Mittelschichten, entfremdete jedoch jüngere Mitglieder, die angesichts wachsender Ungleichheit tiefgreifende Reformen verlangten. || The party leadership's centrism reassured the urban middle classes but alienated younger members who demanded far-reaching reforms in the face of growing inequality.
NOTE: The legacy headword is accurate but omitted the pedagogically useful masculine article.
074 the Dissent => der Dissens | alts=der politische Widerspruch | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Dissens
REG: Dissens is formal analytical vocabulary for lack of agreement; political Widerspruch is more transparent and foregrounds expressed opposition.
TRAP: Dissens names disagreement or lack of consensus; Dissidenz more readily foregrounds a dissident position or milieu and is not a mechanical substitute.
EX: Offener Dissens wurde innerhalb der Einheitspartei als Illoyalität behandelt, sodass selbst moderate Einwände nur noch in vertraulichen Sitzungen geäußert wurden. || Open dissent was treated as disloyalty within the ruling unity party, so even moderate objections were voiced only in confidential meetings.
NOTE: The isolated English can denote either disagreement or organized opposition. The legacy Dissens is retained for the former, while the example fixes a political context.
077 the Popular unity => die Unidad Popular (UP) | alts=das Bündnis Unidad Popular | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Unidad Popular
REG: Historical proper name for the Chilean left-wing electoral alliance; spell out the Spanish name at first mention and use UP thereafter.
TRAP: Literal Volkseinheit obscures the established proper name; German historical writing normally retains Spanish Unidad Popular for the Chilean coalition.
EX: Der Kandidat der Unidad Popular gewann 1970 die Präsidentschaftswahl, verfügte jedoch im Kongress über keine eigene Mehrheit und blieb auf fragile Absprachen angewiesen. || The Popular Unity candidate won the 1970 presidential election but lacked a majority of his own in Congress and remained dependent on fragile agreements.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back. The source is not explicitly labeled as a proper name, but the neighboring Chile-specific items—copper, Christian Democracy, expropriation and land reform—strongly support Unidad Popular. Self-review assigned the election victory precisely to the alliance's candidate rather than to the coalition itself.
078 the Divestment => die Desinvestition | alts=der Rückzug von Investitionen | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Desinvestition
REG: Formal economics term for reducing invested capital, for example by disposing of assets or declining to replace productive capacity.
TRAP: German Desinvestition is broader than the sale of one asset; use Veräußerung for that transaction and Kapitalabzug when capital flight is specifically intended.
EX: Die anhaltende Unsicherheit beschleunigte die Desinvestition, weil Unternehmen Gewinne ins Ausland transferierten und überalterte Produktionsanlagen nicht mehr ersetzten. || Persistent uncertainty accelerated disinvestment because companies transferred profits abroad and stopped replacing aging production facilities.
NOTE: The English divestment is underspecified. The legacy and parallel Romance references point to macroeconomic disinvestment, which the example fixes; ethical divestment would need different wording.
079 the Polarization => die Polarisierung | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Polarisierung
REG: Neutral-formal political and social-science term for a process in which positions or groups move toward increasingly opposed poles.
TRAP: In political German, the idiomatic process noun is Polarisierung; Polarisation also exists but is especially associated with technical and physical contexts.
EX: Die politische Polarisierung verschärfte sich, als regierungsnahe Medien jeden Kompromiss als Verrat darstellten und oppositionelle Sender pauschal des Extremismus beschuldigten. || Political polarization intensified as pro-government media portrayed every compromise as betrayal and indiscriminately accused opposition broadcasters of extremism.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card records the register-sensitive contrast with the cross-language cognate Polarisation.
080 the Foreign relations => die Außenbeziehungen | alts=die auswärtigen Beziehungen | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Außenbeziehungen
REG: Neutral-formal diplomatic and international-relations plural for a state's relationships with foreign governments or external actors.
TRAP: Außenpolitik means foreign policy—the state's approach and decisions—not the bilateral or multilateral relationships denoted by Außenbeziehungen.
EX: Die Außenbeziehungen verbesserten sich nach dem Regierungswechsel rasch, obwohl die Streitfragen um enteignete Vermögenswerte und ausstehende Kredite zunächst ungelöst blieben. || Foreign relations improved rapidly after the change of government, although the disputes over expropriated assets and outstanding loans initially remained unresolved.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the important distinction between relations and policy. Self-review replaced articleless Streit with the more idiomatic plural Streitfragen.

