### de b03
084 Developmentalism => der Developmentalismus | alts=der Desarrollismo | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Developmentalismus
REG: Formal development-studies term for a state-led strategy of accelerated national development; Desarrollismo is used especially for its Latin American form.
TRAP: The legacy Entwicklungshilfe means development aid, not developmentalism; the latter is a doctrine or policy model rather than external assistance.
EX: Der Developmentalismus versprach, durch staatlich gelenkte Industrialisierung und Importsubstitution die Abhängigkeit Brasiliens zu verringern, verschärfte jedoch regionale und soziale Ungleichheiten. || Developmentalism promised to reduce Brazil's dependency through state-directed industrialization and import substitution, but it exacerbated regional and social inequalities.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which mistranslates the concept as development aid. The alternative preserves the Spanish name used for the specifically Latin American programmatic tradition.
085 the Import substitution => die Importsubstitution | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Importsubstitution
REG: Formal economics and development-policy term for replacing imported goods with domestic production, often as part of an industrialization strategy.
TRAP: Importsubstitution is not merely an Importbeschränkung: the former requires domestic production to replace imports, whereas the latter may only restrict trade.
EX: Die Importsubstitution förderte zunächst heimische Industrien, machte sie aber zugleich von staatlichen Krediten, geschützten Märkten und importierten Vorprodukten abhängig. || Import substitution initially promoted domestic industries, but at the same time made them dependent on state loans, protected markets and imported intermediate goods.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card fixes the specialist economic sense and distinguishes the production strategy from a mere import restriction.
087 Tropicalia (referring to the music and art movement) => die Tropicália | alts=der Tropicalismo | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Tropicália
REG: Established cultural-historical name for the Brazilian movement of the late 1960s; Tropicalismo is a common alternative, especially in music history.
TRAP: Preserve the Portuguese proper name and its accent, Tropicália; the explanatory phrase in old_back is not part of the headword.
EX: Die Tropicália verband experimentelle Popmusik mit bildender Kunst und politischer Provokation, bis das Militärregime führende Künstler verhaften oder ins Exil treiben ließ. || Tropicália combined experimental pop music with visual art and political provocation until the military regime had leading artists arrested or driven into exile.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back by supplying the established accented proper name alone rather than appending a translated parenthetical explanation. Self-review replaced zentrale with the idiomatic führende for leading artists.
088 Clandestine => klandestin | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=Klandestine
REG: Written-formal adjective used particularly for covert political, publishing or intelligence activity; heimlich is broader and less institutional.
TRAP: The prompt is adjectival: German uses lowercase klandestin and inflects it, as in klandestine Verlage; Die klandestine is an incomplete noun phrase.
EX: Klandestine Verlage verbreiteten verbotene Essays über Universitätsnetzwerke, obwohl Geheimdienste Druckereien überwachten und bereits der Besitz einzelner Ausgaben Haftstrafen nach sich ziehen konnte. || Clandestine publishers distributed banned essays through university networks, although intelligence services monitored printing presses and possession of individual issues could already result in prison sentences.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which capitalizes the adjective, adds an article and leaves the resulting phrase without a noun.
090 the Industrialists => die Industriellen | alts=die Industrieunternehmer | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Industrielle
REG: Neutral-formal plural for owners or leading business figures in industry; Industrieunternehmer explicitly foregrounds their entrepreneurial role.
TRAP: Industrieller is a substantivized adjective: ein Industrieller, mit einem Industriellen, die Industriellen; Industriearbeiter instead denotes industrial workers.
EX: Einflussreiche Industrielle unterstützten die neue Regierung, weil sie niedrige Löhne, günstige Kredite und wirksamen Schutz vor ausländischer Konkurrenz erwarteten. || Influential industrialists supported the new government because they expected low wages, favorable loans and effective protection from foreign competition.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; the card adds the substantivized-adjective morphology and separates industrialists from industrial workers. Self-review replaced stable wages and determined protection with the historically sharper niedrige Löhne and idiomatic wirksamen Schutz.
091 the Military governance => die Militärherrschaft | alts=die Herrschaft des Militärs | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Militärherrschaft
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political term for a condition or period of military rule; Militärregierung more narrowly names the governing administration.
TRAP: Old_back changes governance into Regierung: Militärherrschaft denotes military rule, while Militärregierung can refer to the executive body exercising it.
EX: Unter der Militärherrschaft wurden Parlamente entmachtet, oppositionelle Parteien eingeschränkt und zentrale wirtschaftspolitische Entscheidungen zunehmend nicht gewählten Technokraten übertragen. || Under military rule, parliaments were stripped of power, opposition parties were restricted and key economic-policy decisions were increasingly entrusted to unelected technocrats.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back by distinguishing the system of military rule from a military government as an institutional actor.
093 the Amazon => Amazonien | alts=das Amazonasgebiet | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Amazonien
REG: Neutral geographical term for the Amazon region; Amazonasgebiet is an explicit analytical alternative, while der Amazonas normally identifies the river.
TRAP: English the Amazon is ambiguous; in this Brazilian regional context use Amazonien or das Amazonasgebiet, not automatically der Amazonas, which primarily names the river.
EX: Amazonien galt der Militärregierung als unerschlossener Wirtschaftsraum, weshalb sie Straßenbau, Bergbau und staatlich geförderte Siedlungsprojekte ohne Rücksicht auf indigene Territorien vorantrieb. || The Amazon region appeared to the military government as an undeveloped economic space, so it advanced roadbuilding, mining and state-sponsored settlement projects without regard for Indigenous territories.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back for the intended regional sense. The English could denote the river, but the Brazilian context and the Romance source references select the region. Self-review made Militärregierung singular to keep the example specifically Brazilian.
095 Cinema Novo => das Cinema Novo | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Cinema Novo
REG: Established film-historical proper name for the Brazilian movement that emerged in the late 1950s; German normally uses it with a neuter article.
TRAP: Do not translate the proper name as Kino Novo; Novo Cinema names a separate Portuguese movement, so word order is also significant.
EX: Das Cinema Novo stellte soziale Ungleichheit mit knappen Mitteln und einer radikalen Bildsprache dar und widersetzte sich damit den kommerziellen Mustern importierter Unterhaltungsfilme. || Cinema Novo portrayed social inequality with limited resources and a radical visual language, thereby resisting the commercial patterns of imported entertainment films.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which partially translates and thereby corrupts the established Portuguese proper name. Self-review changed the abstract plural Bildsprachen to the more coherent einer radikalen Bildsprache.
097 Autarky => die Autarkie | alts=die wirtschaftliche Selbstversorgung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Autarkie
REG: Formal economics and political-history term for the policy or condition of economic self-sufficiency; the alternative is explanatory rather than terminological.
TRAP: Autarkie means economic self-sufficiency and must not be confused with Autokratie, autocracy; the corresponding adjective is autark.
EX: Die angestrebte Autarkie blieb unerreichbar, da selbst stark abgeschottete Volkswirtschaften für ihre Industrialisierung auf Maschinen, Energieimporte und ausländische Technologie angewiesen waren. || The desired autarky remained unattainable because even heavily insulated economies depended on machinery, energy imports and foreign technology for their industrialization.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the high-value distinction from the near-form Autokratie.
098 the Dependency theory => die Dependenztheorie | alts=die Abhängigkeitstheorie | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Dependenztheorie
REG: Formal specialist term in development sociology and political economy; the plural Dependenztheorien is common when distinguishing its different approaches.
TRAP: German uses the closed compound Dependenztheorie, not an English-style *Dependency-Theorie; Dependenz here denotes structural dependence, not an individual dependency.
EX: Die Dependenztheorie erklärte Unterentwicklung nicht als vorübergehenden Rückstand, sondern als Folge asymmetrischer Handelsbeziehungen zwischen industriellen Zentren und peripheren Volkswirtschaften. || Dependency theory explained underdevelopment not as a temporary lag but as a consequence of asymmetric trade relations between industrial centers and peripheral economies.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the example fixes the Latin American structural-development sense rather than a generic use of dependency.
099 the Technocrats => die Technokraten | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Technokraten
REG: Neutral-formal political and sociological plural, often critical when expertise is used to present contested choices as politically neutral necessities.
TRAP: Technokrat is a weak masculine noun: der Technokrat, den Technokraten and die Technokraten; do not use *die Technokrats under English influence.
EX: Die Technokraten begründeten Lohnkontrollen und Haushaltskürzungen mit ökonomischer Sachlogik, obwohl ihre Modelle die politischen Kosten wachsender Armut weitgehend ausblendeten. || The technocrats justified wage controls and budget cuts by invoking economic logic, although their models largely ignored the political costs of growing poverty.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; the card supplies the weak-noun morphology absent from the isolated gloss.
100 the Favelas => die Favelas | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Favelas
REG: Neutral Brazilian social-geography term in informed reporting; Armenviertel is reductive, and not every informal settlement is a favela.
TRAP: The German singular is die Favela and the plural is die Favelas, not *Favelen; avoid treating the term as a synonym for every poor neighborhood.
EX: Viele Favelas entstanden auf ökologisch riskanten Flächen, weil formelle Wohnungsmärkte einkommensschwache Familien ausschlossen und kommunale Infrastruktur dem Bevölkerungswachstum nicht folgte. || Many favelas arose on environmentally hazardous land because formal housing markets excluded low-income families and municipal infrastructure did not keep pace with population growth.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; the card records its German number morphology and rejects the overly broad equation with Armenviertel.
101 Bureaucratic-authoritarian => bürokratisch-autoritär | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=bürokratisch-autoritäre
REG: Formal political-science adjective, especially associated with analyses of Latin American regimes led by military officers and technocratic elites.
TRAP: The item is an adjective: write bürokratisch-autoritär in lowercase and inflect the second element; Die bürokratisch-autoritäre is an incomplete noun phrase.
EX: Das bürokratisch-autoritäre Regime stützte sich auf Militärs und Technokraten, schloss breite Bevölkerungsschichten politisch aus und versprach zugleich beschleunigte Industrialisierung. || The bureaucratic-authoritarian regime relied on military officers and technocrats, politically excluded broad sections of the population and simultaneously promised accelerated industrialization.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which capitalizes the adjective, adds an article and omits the noun required by that inflected form.
104 the Caudillo => der Caudillo | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Caudillo
REG: Historical and political term, often critical, for a personalist leader or strongman in Spain or Latin America; it is not simply a synonym for every dictator.
TRAP: German retains the Spanish loanword with masculine gender and forms the plural Caudillos; Diktator is broader and loses the tradition of personalist patronage.
EX: Der Caudillo band regionale Eliten durch Patronage an seine Person und schwächte Institutionen, die seiner informellen Macht dauerhafte Grenzen hätten setzen können. || The caudillo bound regional elites to himself through patronage and weakened institutions that could have placed lasting limits on his informal power.
NOTE: The legacy headword is accurate; the card adds the historical scope and avoids flattening the term into the broader Diktator.
105 Corporatism => der Korporatismus | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Korporatismus
REG: Formal political-science term for organizing interest representation through recognized functional associations; its precise institutional form varies by theory and period.
TRAP: Korporatismus does not mean rule by commercial corporations: English corporation is normally Unternehmen or Konzern in that business sense.
EX: Der staatliche Korporatismus band Gewerkschaften und Arbeitgeberverbände in kontrollierte Verhandlungsgremien ein, begrenzte jedoch ihre organisatorische Autonomie und unabhängige politische Mobilisierung. || State corporatism incorporated trade unions and employers' associations into controlled negotiating bodies but limited their organizational autonomy and independent political mobilization.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the crucial distinction from corporate commercial power.
108 the Surveillance => die Überwachung | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Überwachung
REG: Neutral-formal institutional term for systematic monitoring by state, police, intelligence or technical systems; context determines whether it is covert.
TRAP: French surveillance and its Romance cognates should not be imported into ordinary political German; outside specialist uses such as epidemiology, the standard term is Überwachung.
EX: Die digitale Überwachung oppositioneller Netzwerke ermöglichte präventive Festnahmen, schuf aber zugleich riesige Datenbestände, deren politische Deutung häufig von Vorurteilen geprägt war. || Digital surveillance of opposition networks enabled preventive arrests but also created vast datasets whose political interpretation was often shaped by prejudice.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
111 the Economic miracle => das Wirtschaftswunder | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Wirtschaftswunder
REG: Journalistic and historical label for unusually rapid economic growth; in critical analysis it is often qualified as so-called because it embeds an evaluative narrative.
TRAP: Established historical labels use the compound Wirtschaftswunder, not a literal *ökonomisches Wunder; the term praises growth and can conceal its distributional costs.
EX: Das brasilianische Wirtschaftswunder steigerte Produktion und Exporte rasant, doch sinkende Reallöhne und wachsende regionale Ungleichheit relativierten die offizielle Erfolgserzählung. || The Brazilian economic miracle rapidly increased production and exports, but falling real wages and growing regional inequality qualified the official success story.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the example makes the Brazilian historical referent and the label's evaluative force explicit.
112 the Debt => die Verschuldung | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Verschuldung
REG: Neutral-formal macroeconomic term for a state or process of indebtedness; Schulden denotes the liabilities themselves, while Schuldenstand quantifies their total.
TRAP: Do not confuse Verschuldung with singular Schuld, which can mean moral guilt or one obligation; the isolated English debt leaves this distinction implicit.
EX: Die wachsende Verschuldung zwang die Regierung zu Verhandlungen mit internationalen Gläubigern, deren Kreditauflagen tief in die Sozialpolitik und die öffentliche Investitionsplanung eingriffen. || Growing debt forced the government into negotiations with international creditors whose lending conditions intruded deeply into social policy and public investment planning.
NOTE: The English prompt is underspecified; the legacy rendering and the regional macroeconomic sequence support aggregate indebtedness, which the example fixes explicitly. Self-review supplied parallel articles before both policy domains.
114 the Geopolitics => die Geopolitik | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Geopolitik
REG: Formal academic and media term for the interaction of geography, power and strategy; it can also critically name a strategic way of framing conflicts.
TRAP: English geopolitics ends in -s, but German normally uses the singular collective Geopolitik rather than a mechanically copied plural *Geopolitiken.
EX: Die Geopolitik des Kalten Krieges verwandelte regionale Konflikte in globale Stellvertreterkrisen, deren lokale Ursachen in westlichen Analysen oft zweitrangig erschienen. || Cold War geopolitics turned regional conflicts into global proxy crises whose local causes often appeared secondary in Western analyses.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
115 the Infrastructure => die Infrastruktur | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Infrastruktur
REG: Neutral policy and economics collective noun; the plural Infrastrukturen is appropriate mainly when distinct systems, sectors or national networks are being compared.
TRAP: English and Romance plurals can encourage overuse of Infrastrukturen; German normally uses singular Infrastruktur for a place's transport, utility and public-service base collectively.
EX: Die staatliche Infrastruktur verband Rohstoffregionen mit Exporthäfen, während ländliche Gemeinden weiterhin kaum Zugang zu Strom, Gesundheitsversorgung oder befestigten Straßen hatten. || State infrastructure connected resource-producing regions to export ports, while rural communities continued to have little access to electricity, healthcare or paved roads.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
116 Mestizo => der Mestize | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Mestize
REG: Historical or explicitly metalinguistic label; it derives from colonial racial classification and is now widely regarded as discriminatory, so avoid it as an unmarked present-day designation.
TRAP: The German masculine noun is Mestize, plural Mestizen, with feminine Mestizin; Spanish mestizaje instead names mixing or the ideology built around it, not an individual.
EX: Koloniale Verwaltungen verwendeten die Kategorie Mestize, um Menschen hierarchisch einzuordnen, deren Herkunft und Selbstverständnis sich solchen starren Zuschreibungen vielfach entzogen. || Colonial administrations used the category mestizo to rank people hierarchically, even though their origins and self-understanding often eluded such rigid labels.
NOTE: The source is genuinely ambiguous: the English may be a noun or adjective, while its Romance references conflict in number and even select mestizaje. The capitalized English and German legacy plural support the person noun; the example deliberately historicizes it.
117 the Economic disparity => das wirtschaftliche Gefälle | alts=die wirtschaftliche Ungleichheit | lexical-expression | CLOZE=wirtschaftliche Gefälle
REG: Neutral-formal analytical expression for a measurable gap between regions or groups; wirtschaftliche Ungleichheit is broader and need not explicitly compare two poles.
TRAP: Gefälle is neuter and commonly takes zwischen plus dative when the two sides are named; here its abstract sense is disparity, not a physical slope.
EX: Das wirtschaftliche Gefälle zwischen Küstenmetropolen und ländlichem Hinterland vertiefte politische Konflikte, weil öffentliche Investitionen weiterhin den bereits prosperierenden Regionen zugutekamen. || The economic disparity between coastal metropolises and the rural hinterland deepened political conflicts because public investment continued to benefit regions that were already prospering.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back for an explicit comparison between regions; wirtschaftliche Ungleichheit remains a valid broader alternative.
118 Afro-Brazilian => afrobrasilianisch | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=Afrobrasilianische
REG: Neutral academic and cultural adjective for people, practices or traditions in Brazil with African ancestry or origins; identity-sensitive contexts may require more specific wording.
TRAP: The standard compact adjective is lowercase afrobrasilianisch and inflects normally; old_back incorrectly capitalizes the isolated adjective, while hyphenation varies for emphasis or clarity.
EX: Afrobrasilianische Künstler verbanden religiöse Traditionen, populäre Musik und politische Forderungen zu Ausdrucksformen, die das offizielle Bild einer konfliktfreien Nationalkultur grundlegend infrage stellten. || Afro-Brazilian artists combined religious traditions, popular music and political demands into forms of expression that fundamentally challenged the official image of a conflict-free national culture.
NOTE: Materially normalizes old_back to the lowercase unhyphenated citation form; the example supplies the required sentence-initial capitalization and adjectival inflection.
119 the Indigenous rights => die Rechte indigener Völker | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Rechte indigener Völker
REG: Current formal human-rights and policy wording, especially for collective rights to territory, culture and self-determination.
TRAP: Ureinwohner is often reductive or othering in current institutional prose; indigene Völker is the established rights-bearing collective, with lowercase inflected indigener.
EX: Die Rechte indigener Völker blieben trotz verfassungsrechtlicher Garantien prekär, sobald Bergbaukonzessionen, Staudämme oder militärische Sicherheitsinteressen ihre Territorien berührten. || The rights of Indigenous peoples remained precarious despite constitutional guarantees whenever mining concessions, dams or military security interests affected their territories.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back by replacing the dated Rechte der Ureinwohner with the current institutional term for the collective rights of Indigenous peoples.

exec
/bin/bash -lc ".venv/bin/python - <<'PY'
import json,re
p='idiomatic/grammar/data/exercises2/batches/output/de_cold_war_vocab_b04_notes.json'
for n in json.load(open(p)):
 t=' | '.join(re.findall(r'\\{\\{c1::(.*?)\\}\\}',n['cloze']))
 print(f\"{n['id'][-3:]} {n['en']} => {n['tl']} | alts={'; '.join(n['alts']) or '—'} | {n['category']} | CLOZE={t}\\nREG: {n['register']}\\nTRAP: {n['trap'] or '—'}\\nEX: {n['example_tl']} || {n['example_en']}\\nNOTE: {n['note'] or '—'}\")
PY" in /home/admin/projects/idiomatic
 succeeded in 0ms:
121 the Patron-client relations => die Patron-Klient-Beziehungen | alts=die Patronagebeziehungen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Patron-Klient-Beziehungen
REG: Formal political-science term for asymmetric reciprocal ties in which a patron grants resources or protection in return for loyalty and support.
TRAP: Patronage alone can mean the distribution of favors; Patron-Klient-Beziehungen explicitly names the durable relationship and takes hyphens between its nominal components.
EX: Die Patron-Klient-Beziehungen sicherten lokalen Machthabern Loyalität, während staatliche Aufträge, Kredite und Ämter gezielt an politisch abhängige Gefolgsleute verteilt wurden. || Patron-client relations secured loyalty for local power holders, while state contracts, loans and offices were deliberately distributed to politically dependent followers.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the exchange structure that distinguishes the term from isolated favoritism.
122 the Amazon deforestation => die Entwaldung Amazoniens | alts=die Abholzung im Amazonasgebiet | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Entwaldung Amazoniens
REG: Neutral-formal environmental and policy expression; Entwaldung covers lasting forest loss, whereas Abholzung focuses more narrowly on tree clearing.
TRAP: Der Amazonas normally names the river; the region is Amazonien or das Amazonasgebiet, so Abholzung des Amazonas is semantically misleading.
EX: Die Entwaldung Amazoniens beschleunigte sich, als Straßenbau, Viehzucht und Landspekulation immer tiefer in zuvor schwer zugängliche Waldgebiete vordrangen. || Amazon deforestation accelerated as road construction, cattle ranching and land speculation pushed ever deeper into previously hard-to-reach forest areas.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, whose genitive can make the river rather than the Amazon region the object of deforestation.
123 the Integration => die Integration in + Akk. | alts=die Eingliederung in + Akk. | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Integration in
REG: Neutral-formal institutional term; Integration stresses incorporation into a larger political, economic or social structure.
TRAP: German expresses the destination with in plus accusative: die Integration in einen Staatenbund, not a calque with *zu or *mit.
EX: Die Integration in regionale Bündnisse versprach wirtschaftliche Stabilität, schränkte aber den außenpolitischen Spielraum jener Staaten ein, die zwischen den Machtblöcken lavierten. || Integration into regional alliances promised economic stability but restricted the foreign-policy room for maneuver of states navigating between the power blocs.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
124 the Literacy => die Lese- und Schreibkompetenz | alts=die Schriftsprachkompetenz | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Lese- und Schreibkompetenz
REG: Neutral educational and policy expression for the acquired ability to read and write; Schriftsprachkompetenz is more technical and can imply a broader competence continuum.
TRAP: Alphabetisierung usually denotes the process or policy of teaching literacy, not the ability already possessed; Alphabetisierungsgrad is a population rate.
EX: Eine höhere Lese- und Schreibkompetenz erweiterte den Zugang zu politischen Informationen, beseitigte jedoch weder soziale Ungleichheit noch die staatliche Kontrolle der Presse. || Higher literacy expanded access to political information but eliminated neither social inequality nor state control of the press.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back by selecting the ability rather than the process of teaching people to read and write.
130 the Evangelical rise => das Erstarken evangelikaler Kirchen | alts=der Aufstieg evangelikaler Bewegungen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Erstarken evangelikaler Kirchen
REG: Neutral-formal sociological expression for the growing membership and influence of evangelical churches or movements, particularly in Latin America.
TRAP: Evangelikal refers to evangelical currents, while evangelisch is the broad German label for Protestant churches and is not equivalent here.
EX: Das Erstarken evangelikaler Kirchen veränderte Brasiliens Parteienlandschaft, weil gut organisierte Gemeinden zunehmend Kandidaten unterstützten und eigene Mediennetzwerke aufbauten. || The rise of evangelical churches changed Brazil's party landscape as well-organized congregations increasingly supported candidates and built their own media networks.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back: Aufstieg der Evangelikalen foregrounds people, while the source context concerns the institutional growth of churches and movements.
131 the Illiteracy => der Analphabetismus | alts=die fehlende Lese- und Schreibkompetenz | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Analphabetismus
REG: Neutral-formal policy term for the social phenomenon; person-centered prose often prefers descriptions of limited or absent literacy skills.
TRAP: Analphabetismus names the phenomenon, whereas Analphabet or Analphabetin labels a person; funktionaler Analphabetismus refers specifically to severely limited everyday literacy.
EX: Der Analphabetismus blieb in ländlichen Regionen hoch, obwohl nationale Bildungsprogramme mehr Schulen eröffneten und die Schulpflicht formal längst ausgeweitet worden war. || Illiteracy remained high in rural regions although national education programs had opened more schools and compulsory schooling had formally been extended long before.
NOTE: Selects the established policy term over legacy Analphabetentum and records a more person-centered descriptive alternative.
132 the Frontier expansion => die Ausweitung der Agrargrenze | alts=die Expansion der landwirtschaftlichen Nutzungsgrenze | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Ausweitung der Agrargrenze
REG: Formal development and environmental-history term for agriculture and settlement advancing into previously uncultivated territory.
TRAP: Here frontier is a moving agricultural settlement boundary, not a national border; Ausdehnung der Grenzen wrongly suggests territorial enlargement of a state.
EX: Die Ausweitung der Agrargrenze verdrängte indigene Gemeinschaften, beschleunigte die Entwaldung und machte abgelegene Regionen stärker von globalen Rohstoffpreisen abhängig. || Frontier expansion displaced Indigenous communities, accelerated deforestation and made remote regions more dependent on global commodity prices.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which interprets frontier as a state's borders and loses the Latin American land-use concept.
133 the Detente => die Entspannungspolitik | alts=die Entspannung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Entspannungspolitik
REG: Established historical and diplomatic term for policies intended to ease East-West tensions; Entspannung can also name the resulting phase or process.
TRAP: The French loanword Détente occurs in specialist German, but Entspannungspolitik is the normal production term when the policy rather than merely a calmer condition is meant.
EX: Die Entspannungspolitik schuf neue Gesprächskanäle zwischen den Supermächten, ohne ihre ideologische Rivalität oder die gewaltsamen Stellvertreterkonflikte in der Peripherie zu beenden. || Détente created new channels of communication between the superpowers without ending their ideological rivalry or the violent proxy conflicts on the periphery.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the alternative covers cases where English détente refers to the broader period or condition rather than a deliberate policy.
134 the Espionage => die Spionage | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Spionage
REG: Neutral-formal security and historical term for covertly obtaining protected information; it normally carries a hostile or unlawful implication.
TRAP: Spionage is the activity, while Geheimdienst or Nachrichtendienst names an organization; German idiom is Spionage betreiben, not mechanically *Spionage machen.
EX: Die Spionage verlagerte sich zunehmend auf technische Aufklärung, doch menschliche Quellen blieben entscheidend, wenn politische Absichten hinter militärischen Signalen erkannt werden sollten. || Espionage increasingly shifted toward technical intelligence, but human sources remained crucial when political intentions behind military signals had to be discerned.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
135 the Proxy war => der Stellvertreterkrieg | alts=der Proxykrieg | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Stellvertreterkrieg
REG: Established political and historical term; Proxykrieg is a newer anglicism used in current commentary but is less traditional.
TRAP: A Stellvertreterkrieg is fought by local parties backed by outside powers; Stellvertreter here does not mean a formally appointed deputy.
EX: Der Stellvertreterkrieg in Angola verband lokale Machtkämpfe mit globaler Systemkonkurrenz, während ausländische Mächte Waffen, Berater und finanzielle Unterstützung bereitstellten. || The proxy war in Angola linked local power struggles with global systemic rivalry while foreign powers supplied weapons, advisers and financial support.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card distinguishes the established German compound from its literal nonpolitical reading.
137 the Nuclear arms race => das nukleare Wettrüsten | alts=das atomare Wettrüsten | lexical-expression | CLOZE=nukleare Wettrüsten
REG: Neutral-formal historical and security-policy expression; nuklear is standard in contemporary analytical prose, while atomar remains common in established compounds.
TRAP: English arms race maps idiomatically to Wettrüsten, not to a literal *Rüstungsrennen; the noun is neuter and normally singular.
EX: Das nukleare Wettrüsten band enorme Ressourcen, erhöhte das Risiko von Fehlkalkulationen und machte technische Frühwarnsysteme zu einem zentralen Bestandteil strategischer Planung. || The nuclear arms race consumed enormous resources, increased the risk of miscalculation and made technical early-warning systems a central component of strategic planning.
NOTE: The legacy atomare Wettrüsten is also idiomatic; the main form adopts the currently prevalent analytical adjective nuklear.
139 the Domino theory => die Dominotheorie | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Dominotheorie
REG: Established historical and political-science label, often used critically for a strategic assumption in United States Cold War policy.
TRAP: German normally forms the closed compound Dominotheorie; the concept concerns a presumed chain of political regime changes, not literal domino pieces.
EX: Die Dominotheorie prägte Washingtons Interventionen in Südostasien, weil Entscheidungsträger lokale Revolutionen als zwangsläufige Glieder einer weltweiten kommunistischen Expansion deuteten. || Domino theory shaped Washington's interventions in Southeast Asia because decision-makers interpreted local revolutions as inevitable links in a worldwide communist expansion.
NOTE: Self-review normalized the acceptable legacy hyphenation to the conventional closed German compound and made the disputed causal premise explicit in the example.
140 the Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD) => die gegenseitige gesicherte Zerstörung (MAD) | alts=die wechselseitig gesicherte Zerstörung | lexical-expression | CLOZE=gegenseitige gesicherte Zerstörung
REG: Formal nuclear-strategy term; German discussions usually retain the English acronym MAD even when translating the full expression.
TRAP: Assured here means made certain by survivable retaliation, so gesichert is correct; the legacy zugesichert wrongly suggests that destruction was verbally promised.
EX: Die gegenseitige gesicherte Zerstörung beruhte auf der Fähigkeit beider Supermächte, selbst nach einem nuklearen Erstschlag noch vernichtend zurückzuschlagen. || Mutually assured destruction rested on both superpowers' ability to retaliate devastatingly even after suffering a nuclear first strike.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back to the established German strategic term; the headword retains MAD as the conventional identifying acronym.
142 the Deterrence => die Abschreckung | alts=die Deterrenz | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Abschreckung
REG: Standard security-policy term for preventing action through a credible threat of unacceptable costs; Deterrenz is a rarer specialist synonym.
TRAP: Abschreckung is prevention through threatened costs, whereas Abwehr means defending against an attack once attempted; the two are not interchangeable.
EX: Die nukleare Abschreckung setzte voraus, dass jede Seite die gegnerische Vergeltungsfähigkeit für glaubwürdig hielt und zugleich unbeabsichtigte Eskalationen vermeiden konnte. || Nuclear deterrence presupposed that each side considered the adversary's retaliatory capability credible and could simultaneously avoid unintended escalation.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the example fixes the strategic sense rather than the everyday act of frightening someone off.
143 the Non-aligned movement => die Bewegung der Blockfreien | alts=die Blockfreienbewegung | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Bewegung der Blockfreien
REG: Established name in diplomatic and historical prose for the international movement founded by states outside the Cold War alliance blocs.
TRAP: Blockfrei does not necessarily mean neutral or equally distant from both blocs; members could take strong positions while rejecting formal bloc alignment.
EX: Die Bewegung der Blockfreien beanspruchte außenpolitische Eigenständigkeit, obwohl ihre Mitglieder wirtschaftlich und militärisch sehr unterschiedlich eng an die Machtblöcke gebunden blieben. || The Non-Aligned Movement claimed foreign-policy autonomy even though its members remained tied to the power blocs to widely differing economic and military degrees.
NOTE: The legacy rendering and the compact alternative are both established; self-review removed any implication that non-alignment required political neutrality.
144 the Satellite state => der Satellitenstaat | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Satellitenstaat
REG: Historical and political term, usually critical, for a formally sovereign state under the decisive influence of a stronger power.
TRAP: Satellitenstaat is an evaluative dependency label, not a neutral constitutional category and not a state associated with spaceflight or communications satellites.
EX: Als Satellitenstaat blieb die Republik formal souverän, doch ihre Außenpolitik, Sicherheitsorgane und wirtschaftlichen Prioritäten wurden maßgeblich von Moskau bestimmt. || As a satellite state, the republic remained formally sovereign, but its foreign policy, security organs and economic priorities were largely determined by Moscow.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the register warning prevents the historical label from being presented as a value-neutral legal status.
145 the Sphere of influence => die Einflusssphäre | alts=der Einflussbereich | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Einflusssphäre
REG: Neutral-formal geopolitical term; Einflussbereich is broader and can also refer to an individual's or institution's ordinary remit.
TRAP: Use the lexicalized compound Einflusssphäre rather than the English-shaped *Sphäre des Einflusses; the influencing power commonly follows in the genitive.
EX: Die Einflusssphäre einer Großmacht entstand nicht nur durch Militärbündnisse, sondern auch durch Kredite, Parteikontakte, Geheimdienstkooperation und wirtschaftliche Abhängigkeiten. || A great power's sphere of influence arose not only through military alliances but also through loans, party contacts, intelligence cooperation and economic dependencies.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card adds the productive genitive pattern and distinguishes the geopolitical compound from broader Einflussbereich.
146 the Defectors => die Überläufer | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Überläufer
REG: Historical and intelligence term for people who abandon one side for an opposing side; it can sound judgmental from the abandoned side's perspective.
TRAP: The underlying separable verb is überlaufen and forms the perfect with sein: er ist zum Gegner übergelaufen; Aussteiger does not imply changing sides.
EX: Die Überläufer lieferten westlichen Diensten wertvolle Informationen, doch ihre Aussagen wurden häufig von Eigeninteressen, Erinnerungslücken und Erwartungen der Befrager geprägt. || The defectors provided Western services with valuable information, but their statements were often shaped by self-interest, memory gaps and interviewers' expectations.
NOTE: The legacy plural is accurate; the card supplies the verbal construction and flags the term's perspective-dependent evaluation.
147 the Intelligence => die nachrichtendienstliche Aufklärung | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=nachrichtendienstliche Aufklärung
REG: Formal security term for the collection and analysis side of intelligence; German selects different words for the activity, its products and the responsible organization.
TRAP: English intelligence is polysemous: German Intelligenz means intellect or intelligentsia, while Geheimdienst names an organization rather than the information-gathering activity.
EX: Die nachrichtendienstliche Aufklärung kombinierte abgefangene Funksprüche mit Agentenberichten, damit politische Entscheidungsträger militärische Fähigkeiten und Absichten der Gegenseite getrennt bewerten konnten. || Intelligence work combined intercepted radio messages with agent reports so that political decision-makers could assess the adversary's military capabilities and intentions separately.
NOTE: The English source is genuinely underspecified. This card selects intelligence activity; if an agency is intended, use Nachrichtendienst or Geheimdienst, and for the product use nachrichtendienstliche Erkenntnisse.
149 the Disinformation => die Desinformation | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Desinformation
REG: Neutral-formal media, security and political term for deliberately created or spread false or misleading information.
TRAP: Desinformation conventionally implies intent to deceive; Fehlinformation can be false by mistake and therefore is not an exact substitute.
EX: Die Desinformation zielte darauf, Misstrauen zwischen Verbündeten zu säen, oppositionelle Gruppen zu diskreditieren und die Urheberschaft manipulierter Berichte glaubhaft zu verschleiern. || Disinformation aimed to sow mistrust between allies, discredit opposition groups and plausibly conceal the authorship of manipulated reports.
NOTE: Retained here under the cross-topic duplicate report, which assigns this exact prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB.
151 the Arms control => die Rüstungskontrolle | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Rüstungskontrolle
REG: Established diplomatic and security-policy term for negotiated limits, transparency measures and verification concerning forces or weapon systems.
TRAP: Waffenkontrolle can mean inspecting or controlling weapons, while Abrüstung aims at reduction or elimination; strategic arms control is Rüstungskontrolle.
EX: Die Rüstungskontrolle begrenzte bestimmte Waffensysteme und schuf Inspektionsregeln, ohne notwendigerweise eine umfassende Abrüstung oder das Ende strategischer Konkurrenz anzustreben. || Arms control limited particular weapon systems and created inspection rules without necessarily seeking comprehensive disarmament or an end to strategic competition.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which uses Waffenkontrolle and misses the established strategic distinction between arms control and disarmament.
152 the Space race => der Wettlauf ins All | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Wettlauf ins All
REG: Established journalistic and historical expression for United States-Soviet competition in spaceflight and its associated technological prestige.
TRAP: The conventional directional phrase is Wettlauf ins All; Wettrennen im Weltraum suggests a literal race taking place in space and is not the historical label.
EX: Der Wettlauf ins All verband wissenschaftliche Forschung mit militärischer Raketenentwicklung und verwandelte technische Erfolge in weithin sichtbare Beweise staatlicher Leistungsfähigkeit. || The space race linked scientific research with military missile development and turned technical achievements into highly visible evidence of state capability.
NOTE: Materially replaces the understandable but unidiomatic legacy phrase with the established German historical label.
155 the Nuclear proliferation => die Weiterverbreitung von Kernwaffen | alts=die nukleare Proliferation | lexical-expression | CLOZE=Weiterverbreitung von Kernwaffen
REG: Formal security and nonproliferation-policy expression; the German descriptive form is especially clear in institutional prose, while the cognate is common among specialists.
TRAP: Proliferation is the spread of weapons, material or know-how to additional actors, not simply enlargement of an existing arsenal, which is Aufrüstung.
EX: Die Weiterverbreitung von Kernwaffen gefährdete die strategische Stabilität, weil zusätzliche Atommächte regionale Konflikte verschärfen und bestehende Abschreckungsmodelle unberechenbarer machen konnten. || Nuclear proliferation threatened strategic stability because additional nuclear powers could intensify regional conflicts and make existing deterrence models less predictable.
NOTE: The legacy cognate is valid specialist German; the main rendering selects the clearer institutional expression and the example fixes the spread-to-new-actors sense.
156 the Infiltration => die Unterwanderung | alts=die Infiltration | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Unterwanderung
REG: Political and security term for covert penetration of an organization; Unterwanderung is often accusatory and should be attributed in critical historical prose.
TRAP: In political German, Infiltration can sound technical or operational; Unterwanderung is idiomatic for covert institutional influence but carries the accuser's framing.
EX: Die behauptete Unterwanderung staatlicher Institutionen diente Sicherheitsbehörden häufig als Vorwand, um Gewerkschaften zu überwachen, Berufsverbote zu verhängen und legitime Opposition zu kriminalisieren. || The alleged infiltration of state institutions often served security authorities as a pretext to monitor trade unions, impose employment bans and criminalize legitimate opposition.
NOTE: Materially improves the legacy cognate for the political sense. Self-review added behauptete to keep the example from endorsing a historically loaded allegation.
157 Glasnost (openness) => die Glasnost | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Glasnost
REG: Established historical name for Gorbachev's policy of greater openness, public discussion and access to information in the Soviet Union.
TRAP: Use the capitalized loanword Glasnost for the named reform policy; generic Offenheit explains its meaning but loses the specific historical referent.
EX: Die Glasnost erweiterte den öffentlichen Debattenraum, sodass Journalisten historische Verbrechen thematisierten und Bürger die Glaubwürdigkeit offizieller Darstellungen zunehmend infrage stellten. || Glasnost widened the space for public debate, allowing journalists to address historical crimes and citizens increasingly to question the credibility of official accounts.
NOTE: The legacy headword is accurate; the explanatory parenthesis belongs in the teaching fields rather than in the production headword. Self-review removed a redundant adverb from the example.
158 Perestroika (restructuring) => die Perestroika | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Perestroika
REG: Established historical name for Gorbachev's program of restructuring the Soviet economy and political system.
TRAP: Generic Umstrukturierung is only an explanatory gloss; translating the named Soviet reform this way erases its historical specificity.
EX: Die Perestroika sollte die sowjetische Wirtschaft erneuern, legte jedoch institutionelle Widersprüche offen, die weder administrative Reformen noch begrenzte Marktmechanismen rasch lösen konnten. || Perestroika was intended to renew the Soviet economy but exposed institutional contradictions that neither administrative reforms nor limited market mechanisms could quickly resolve.
NOTE: The legacy headword is accurate; the explanatory parenthesis is omitted from the production form and supplied through the usage fields.
159 the Hotline => der heiße Draht | alts=die Direktverbindung | lexical-expression | CLOZE=heiße Draht
REG: Established journalistic and historical expression for the direct crisis-communication link between governments; Direktverbindung is more neutral and technical.
TRAP: Modern German Hotline usually suggests a public service telephone number; the Cold War link is der heiße Draht and was not necessarily a voice call.
EX: Der heiße Draht zwischen Washington und Moskau sollte in akuten Krisen Missverständnisse verhindern und den Regierungschefs eine rasche, direkte Kommunikation ermöglichen. || The hotline between Washington and Moscow was intended to prevent misunderstandings during acute crises and enable rapid, direct communication between the two leaders.
NOTE: Materially corrects the modern-service connotation of old_back by selecting the established Cold War expression.

