### de b05
163 the Summits => die Gipfeltreffen | alts=die Treffen auf höchster Ebene | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Gipfeltreffen
REG: Neutral-formal diplomatic and journalistic term for meetings between heads of state or government; the plural has no additional ending.
TRAP: A diplomatic summit is normally a Gipfeltreffen; bare Gipfel is possible by metonymy but also denotes a mountain peak and is less explicit as a headword.
EX: Die Gipfeltreffen boten beiden Supermächten Gelegenheit, Rüstungskontrolle zu erörtern, ohne die tiefen Konflikte über Stellvertreterkriege und Einflusszonen zu lösen. || The summits gave both superpowers an opportunity to discuss arms control without resolving the deep conflicts over proxy wars and spheres of influence.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate; the card makes the diplomatic sense and invariant plural explicit.
164 the Nuclear testing => die Kernwaffentests | alts=die Atomwaffentests | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Kernwaffentests
REG: Standard historical and security-policy plural for tests of nuclear weapons; Atomwaffentests is equally established and somewhat more journalistic.
TRAP: Nuclear testing in Cold War history means testing nuclear weapons, so Kernwaffentests is more precise than the formally possible but less explicit Nukleartests.
EX: Die Kernwaffentests verseuchten abgelegene Regionen, während Regierungen gesundheitliche Folgen herunterspielten und betroffene Gemeinschaften jahrzehntelang ohne angemessene Entschädigung ließen. || Nuclear testing contaminated remote regions while governments played down the health consequences and left affected communities without adequate compensation for decades.
NOTE: Sharpens old_back by naming nuclear weapons explicitly; Nukleartests remains understandable in a firmly established weapons context.
167 the Conventional forces => die konventionellen Streitkräfte | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=konventionellen Streitkräfte
REG: Established security-policy term for armed forces and capabilities other than nuclear forces, with the precise scope depending on the strategic context.
TRAP: Konventionell here contrasts with nuklear and does not merely mean customary or traditional; the adjective remains lowercase inside the noun phrase.
EX: Die konventionellen Streitkräfte blieben trotz nuklearer Abschreckung zentral, weil Bündnisse glaubwürdige Verteidigungspläne für begrenzte Konflikte und regionale Krisen benötigten. || Conventional forces remained central despite nuclear deterrence because alliances needed credible defense plans for limited conflicts and regional crises.
NOTE: Self-review corrected the legacy capitalization of Konventionellen and fixed the non-nuclear strategic sense in the example.
168 the Rearmament => die Wiederbewaffnung | alts=die Wiederaufrüstung | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Wiederbewaffnung
REG: Established historical term for restoring armed forces after demilitarization; Wiederaufrüstung more strongly foregrounds a renewed military buildup.
TRAP: Aufrüstung can expand an existing arsenal, whereas Wiederbewaffnung presupposes that military capacity had previously been removed or severely restricted.
EX: Die Wiederbewaffnung Westdeutschlands spaltete die Öffentlichkeit, da Befürworter Bündnisfähigkeit versprachen und Gegner eine erneute Militarisierung der jungen Republik befürchteten. || The rearmament of West Germany divided the public, as supporters promised alliance capability and opponents feared the renewed militarization of the young republic.
NOTE: Selects the conventional term for the West German historical debate; old_back is valid when renewed arms buildup rather than restoration of armed forces is foregrounded.
170 the Nuclear fallout => der radioaktive Niederschlag | alts=der Fallout | lexical-expression | CLOZE=radioaktive Niederschlag
REG: Standard scientific and policy expression for radioactive particles deposited after a nuclear explosion; Fallout is common in journalistic prose.
TRAP: Fallout here is physical radioactive deposition, not the generic Folgen of an event; nuklearer Fallout is understandable but less idiomatic than radioaktiver Niederschlag.
EX: Der radioaktive Niederschlag atmosphärischer Tests gelangte über weite Entfernungen in Böden und Nahrungsketten, obwohl die Behörden das grenzüberschreitende Risiko zunächst bestritten. || Radioactive fallout from atmospheric tests traveled great distances into soils and food chains, although the authorities initially denied the cross-border risk.
NOTE: Materially replaces the Anglicized legacy phrase with the established descriptive German term. Self-review changed große to the more idiomatic weite Entfernungen.
171 the Nuclear umbrella => der nukleare Schutzschirm | alts=der atomare Schutzschirm | lexical-expression | CLOZE=nukleare Schutzschirm
REG: Established security-policy metaphor for extended nuclear deterrence provided by a nuclear-armed ally.
TRAP: A nuklearer Schutzschirm is a deterrence guarantee, not a Raketenschirm, which denotes systems intended to intercept incoming missiles.
EX: Der nukleare Schutzschirm sollte Verbündete schützen, beruhte jedoch auf der umstrittenen Annahme, Washington werde zur Verteidigung Europas selbst einen Atomkrieg riskieren. || The nuclear umbrella was intended to protect allies but rested on the contested assumption that Washington would itself risk nuclear war to defend Europe.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. Self-review added the missile-defense contrast and replaced the ambiguous phrase einen Gegenschlag riskieren with the clearer Atomkrieg riskieren.
173 Cold peace => der kalte Frieden | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=kalte Frieden
REG: Journalistic and political-analysis expression for formal peace without normalized relations, mutual trust or substantial social contact.
TRAP: In an ordinary noun phrase the adjective is lowercase: ein kalter Frieden; capitalized Kalter Frieden is reserved for title-like or explicitly named use.
EX: Der kalte Frieden zwischen den Nachbarstaaten verhinderte offene Kämpfe, brachte aber weder diplomatische Normalisierung noch regelmäßige Kontakte zwischen ihren Gesellschaften. || The cold peace between the neighboring states prevented open fighting but brought neither diplomatic normalization nor regular contact between their societies.
NOTE: Materially expands the articleless legacy label into a production-ready noun phrase and fixes its ordinary lowercase inflection.
174 to confront => sich jdm./etw. stellen | alts=sich mit etw. auseinandersetzen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=sich der Vergangenheit stellen
REG: Neutral-formal for facing a person, threat, responsibility or difficult issue directly rather than avoiding it.
TRAP: English confront takes a direct object, but this German frame is reflexive and governs the dative; jdn. mit etw. konfrontieren instead presents someone with a fact or problem.
EX: Die Regierung musste sich der Vergangenheit stellen, nachdem freigegebene Akten ihre Beteiligung an geheimen Haftprogrammen und politischen Säuberungen belegten. || The government had to confront the past after declassified files documented its involvement in secret detention programs and political purges.
NOTE: The isolated source is polysemous. This card selects facing a difficult issue; old_back covers confronting someone with something but omits that construction's required complements. Self-review replaced an alternative that did not collocate naturally with Vergangenheit.
175 to oppose => sich gegen etw. aussprechen | alts=etw. ablehnen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=sprachen sich | gegen | aus
REG: Neutral-formal political and diplomatic usage for publicly stating opposition to a proposal, policy or action.
TRAP: The construction is reflexive and takes gegen plus the accusative; widersprechen takes a dative object and more often means contradicting or disagreeing with someone or something said.
EX: Mehrere Diplomaten sprachen sich gegen neue Sanktionen aus, weil diese nach ihrer Einschätzung vor allem die Zivilbevölkerung und unabhängige Medien schwächen würden. || Several diplomats opposed new sanctions because, in their assessment, these would primarily weaken the civilian population and independent media.
NOTE: Narrows the underspecified source to publicly expressed policy opposition; legacy sich widersetzen is stronger and foregrounds active resistance.
176 to align => sich einem Lager anschließen | alts=sich auf die Seite eines Lagers stellen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=sich | anschließen
REG: Neutral-formal foreign-policy usage for joining or taking the side of a geopolitical camp.
TRAP: Sich angleichen means becoming similar or adapting to a standard; political alignment with a camp is sich einem Lager anschließen, with the camp in the dative.
EX: Viele neu unabhängige Staaten wollten sich keinem Lager anschließen und suchten stattdessen durch Blockfreiheit außenpolitischen Spielraum zwischen den rivalisierenden Supermächten. || Many newly independent states did not want to align with either camp and instead sought foreign-policy room for maneuver between the rival superpowers through non-alignment.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which describes assimilation rather than geopolitical alignment.
184 to repress => jdn./etw. unterdrücken | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=unterdrückte
REG: Neutral-critical political usage for systematically preventing people, organizations or dissent from acting or being expressed.
TRAP: English repress changes translation by object: memories are often verdrängt, while an uprising violently suppressed is niedergeschlagen; political actors or networks can be unterdrückt.
EX: Die Geheimpolizei unterdrückte oppositionelle Netzwerke, indem sie Versammlungen auflöste, Druckereien beschlagnahmte und bekannte Organisatoren monatelang ohne Anklage festhielt. || The secret police repressed opposition networks by breaking up meetings, confiscating printing presses and holding prominent organizers for months without charge.
NOTE: The legacy verb is accurate for the political sense; the card is retained because the English verb's psychological and violent-suppression senses require different German choices.
185 to embargo => ein Embargo gegen etw. verhängen | alts=etw. mit einem Embargo belegen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=ein Embargo gegen | verhängen
REG: Formal diplomatic and legal construction for imposing an official ban on trade or specified transfers.
TRAP: German normally uses Embargo with verhängen or mit einem Embargo belegen; embargoisieren is rare and sounds bureaucratic rather than like the unmarked verb.
EX: Der Sicherheitsrat konnte ein Embargo gegen das Regime verhängen, nachdem Inspektoren wiederholt geheime Waffenlieferungen an verbündete Milizen dokumentiert hatten. || The Security Council was able to impose an embargo on the regime after inspectors repeatedly documented secret arms deliveries to allied militias.
NOTE: Materially replaces the marked legacy verb with the idiomatic German support-verb construction. Self-review made the English translation idiomatic without changing its source sense.
187 to negotiate => mit jdm. über etw. verhandeln | alts=Verhandlungen mit jdm. über etw. führen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=verhandelten mit | über
REG: Neutral-formal diplomatic usage; the counterpart follows mit plus dative and the subject of negotiation über plus accusative.
TRAP: Verhandeln does not normally take the desired agreement as a direct object; a concrete treaty or settlement is einen Vertrag beziehungsweise eine Einigung aushandeln.
EX: Die Unterhändler verhandelten mit der Gegenseite über Gefangenenaustausche, während beide Regierungen öffentlich bestritten, überhaupt direkte Gespräche miteinander zu führen. || The negotiators negotiated with the other side over prisoner exchanges while both governments publicly denied that they were holding direct talks at all.
NOTE: The legacy headword is accurate but omits both governed complements needed for productive diplomatic use.
189 to radicalize => sich radikalisieren | alts=radikaler werden | lexical-verb | CLOZE=radikalisierten sich
REG: Neutral analytical in political and social-science prose for an actor's movement toward more radical positions or methods.
TRAP: When the subject becomes more radical, German uses sich radikalisieren; bare jdn. radikalisieren is transitive and presents an external actor as the cause.
EX: Teile der Studentenbewegung radikalisierten sich, als staatliche Gewalt zunahm und legale Möglichkeiten für wirksamen politischen Protest systematisch eingeschränkt wurden. || Parts of the student movement radicalized as state violence increased and legal opportunities for effective political protest were systematically restricted.
NOTE: The source does not specify transitivity. This card selects the common intransitive reading and materially adds the reflexive marker missing from old_back.
193 to exile => jdn. ins Exil verbannen | alts=jdn. ins Exil schicken | lexical-verb | CLOZE=verbannte | ins Exil
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political construction for forcing a person to live outside their country; verbannen foregrounds coercion.
TRAP: Bildungssprachliches exilieren is possible but uncommon and should not be treated as an unmarked cognate; the productive directional phrase is ins Exil.
EX: Das Regime verbannte kritische Schriftsteller ins Exil, entzog ihnen die Staatsbürgerschaft und ließ ihre Werke aus Bibliotheken und Lehrplänen entfernen. || The regime exiled critical writers, stripped them of their citizenship and had their works removed from libraries and curricula.
NOTE: The legacy phrase is valid; the main rendering selects the more concise formal verb and preserves the required directional phrase. Self-review corrected the trap to acknowledge valid but low-frequency exilieren.
195 to disarm => abrüsten | alts=Waffenbestände abbauen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=abrüsten
REG: Standard intransitive security-policy verb for a state or armed actor reducing or eliminating its armaments.
TRAP: Entwaffnen means taking weapons away from a person, unit or immediate opponent; a state reducing its own military arsenal rüstet ab.
EX: Keine Seite wollte vollständig abrüsten, solange sie die gegnerischen Inspektionszusagen für unzureichend hielt und einen verdeckten technologischen Vorsprung befürchtete. || Neither side wanted to disarm completely as long as it considered the adversary's inspection commitments inadequate and feared a covert technological advantage.
NOTE: The isolated source is ambiguous. The Cold War context selects strategic disarmament; old_back is correct only for depriving a person or unit of weapons.
196 to deter => jdn. von etw. abschrecken | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=abschrecken
REG: Standard security-policy construction for preventing an action by credibly threatening unacceptable costs.
TRAP: The person deterred is accusative, while the prevented action follows von plus dative; abhalten is broader and does not itself encode threatened costs.
EX: Glaubwürdige Vergeltungsdrohungen sollten den Gegner von einem Angriff abschrecken, konnten jedoch Fehlwahrnehmungen, technische Störungen und eigenmächtige Entscheidungen lokaler Befehlshaber nicht ausschließen. || Credible threats of retaliation were intended to deter the adversary from attacking but could not rule out misperceptions, technical failures or unilateral decisions by local commanders.
NOTE: The legacy verb is accurate; the card adds the two-object government required for productive strategic use.
197 to escalate => eskalieren | alts=sich zuspitzen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=eskalierte
REG: Neutral analytical usage; the example selects the common intransitive frame in which a crisis or conflict becomes more intense.
TRAP: German permits transitive and intransitive eskalieren, but never reflexive *sich eskalieren; the intransitive perfect allows ist or hat eskaliert, while the transitive perfect uses hat.
EX: Die Krise eskalierte, nachdem ein fehlinterpretiertes Radarsignal Alarm ausgelöst hatte und beide Seiten ihre strategischen Bomber in erhöhte Bereitschaft versetzten. || The crisis escalated after a misinterpreted radar signal triggered an alert and both sides placed their strategic bombers on heightened readiness.
NOTE: The source does not specify transitivity, so the example chooses the intransitive frame. Old_back incorrectly capitalizes the infinitive. Self-review removed an inaccurate claim that transitive eskalieren is disfavored.
198 to proliferate => sich weiterverbreiten | alts=sich ausbreiten | lexical-verb | CLOZE=sich weiterverbreiten
REG: Neutral-formal security-policy usage for weapons, technology or knowledge spreading to additional actors; proliferieren is more specialist.
TRAP: Wuchern describes rampant growth, often of tissue, plants or an uncontrolled phenomenon; it is not the normal verb for nuclear technology spreading between actors.
EX: Westliche Regierungen fürchteten, Kernwaffentechnologie könne sich weiterverbreiten, wenn zivile Nuklearprogramme ohne wirksame Kontrollen sensibles Wissen und Material zugänglich machten. || Western governments feared that nuclear-weapons technology could proliferate if civilian nuclear programs made sensitive knowledge and material accessible without effective controls.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, whose growth metaphor does not express spread to additional actors.
200 to transition => den Übergang zu etw. vollziehen | alts=zu etw. übergehen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=vollzogen den Übergang zur Demokratie
REG: Formal political and historical construction for completing or undergoing a structural transition to another system.
TRAP: English transition is readily used as a verb; German political prose normally uses a support-noun construction, while transitionieren is specialized and unsuitable here.
EX: Mehrere ehemalige Einparteienstaaten vollzogen den Übergang zur Demokratie, ohne dass alte Sicherheitsapparate ihre wirtschaftlichen Netzwerke oder ihren politischen Einfluss vollständig verloren. || Several former one-party states transitioned to democracy without the old security apparatuses completely losing their economic networks or political influence.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which supplies only a noun and therefore does not answer the verbal source prompt.
### de b06
202 to subvert => etw. unterwandern | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=unterwanderte
REG: Neutral-formal political and intelligence usage for covertly gaining influence inside an institution in order to redirect or weaken it.
TRAP: Unterwandern implies covert penetration from within; untergraben means gradually weakening something and does not itself imply placing agents inside it.
EX: Der Geheimdienst unterwanderte über mehrere Jahre unabhängige Gewerkschaften, indem er Informanten in Führungspositionen brachte und interne Konflikte gezielt verschärfte. || Over several years, the intelligence service subverted independent trade unions by placing informants in leadership positions and deliberately intensifying internal conflicts.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. Self-review fixed the covert institutional sense, distinguished it from the retained undermine card and removed infiltrieren as too narrow for a true alternative.
206 to deforest => einen Wald abholzen | alts=einen Wald roden | lexical-verb | CLOZE=abholzen
REG: Neutral environmental, historical and policy usage; abholzen foregrounds tree removal, while roden commonly implies clearing the land more completely.
TRAP: Abholzen is used with a forest or wooded area, whereas individual trees are normally gefällt; roden can additionally imply removing roots to clear land.
EX: Die Militärregierung ließ große Waldflächen abholzen, um neue Siedlungen zu erschließen und zugleich den Rückzugsraum bewaffneter Oppositionsgruppen zu verkleinern. || The military government had large forested areas cleared to open up new settlements while also reducing the refuge available to armed opposition groups.
NOTE: The legacy verb is sound; the production-ready headword supplies a natural object and the card records the useful distinction between logging and fully clearing land.
208 to broadcast => etw. ausstrahlen | alts=etw. senden | lexical-verb | CLOZE=strahlte | aus
REG: Standard media usage for broadcasting recorded or scheduled radio and television content; übertragen is often preferred for a live event or relay.
TRAP: Ausstrahlen is separable: a finite clause has strahlt ... aus and the participle is ausgestrahlt; übertragen is not the default for every broadcast.
EX: Der Sender strahlte die Rede zeitgleich in mehreren Sprachen aus, damit sie auch Hörer jenseits des Eisernen Vorhangs erreichte. || The station broadcast the speech simultaneously in several languages so that it also reached listeners beyond the Iron Curtain.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. Self-review added separable morphology and narrowed the card to media transmission rather than the unrelated sense of spreading widely.
209 to innovate => Innovationen hervorbringen | alts=Neuerungen entwickeln | lexical-expression | CLOZE=brachten | Innovationen hervor
REG: Neutral-formal research and industrial-policy construction for producing substantively new methods, products or technologies.
TRAP: Erneuern means renewing or replacing something already present and does not necessarily mean innovating; bare innovieren is marked specialist or managerial usage.
EX: Unter dem Druck des Wettrüstens brachten staatliche Forschungsinstitute Innovationen hervor, deren zivile Anwendungen später ganze Industriezweige tiefgreifend veränderten. || Under pressure from the arms race, state research institutes produced innovations whose civilian applications later profoundly transformed entire industries.
NOTE: Materially replaces old_back, which means renew. Self-review chose a productive German support-verb expression rather than presenting the marked cognate innovieren as unmarked usage.
210 to impose => jdm. etw. auferlegen | alts=jdm. etw. vorschreiben | lexical-verb | CLOZE=erlegte | auf
REG: Neutral-formal legal and political usage for imposing a requirement, burden or condition on someone; the exact German verb changes with the object.
TRAP: The recipient is dative and the imposed burden accusative; sanctions are verhängt, while jdm. etw. aufzwingen stresses forcing an unwanted thing or view on someone.
EX: Die Besatzungsmacht erlegte der Übergangsregierung strenge Haushaltsauflagen auf, die den Wiederaufbau verzögerten und breite Proteste gegen ausländische Einflussnahme auslösten. || The occupying power imposed strict budgetary conditions on the transitional government, delaying reconstruction and triggering widespread protests against foreign influence.
NOTE: The source is polysemous. This card selects requirements and burdens; old_back is valid only when coercive forcing is foregrounded. Self-review made both German objects explicit.
212 to retaliate => Vergeltung üben | alts=zurückschlagen | lexical-expression | CLOZE=übte Vergeltung
REG: Formal political and military expression for responding to an attack with punitive action; zurückschlagen is more direct and eventive.
TRAP: Sich rächen is usually more personal and emotive; Vergeltung üben is the conventional impersonal policy expression and takes no reflexive pronoun.
EX: Der angegriffene Staat übte Vergeltung, indem er militärische Stützpunkte bombardierte, vermied jedoch bewusst Ziele in dicht besiedelten Gebieten. || The attacked state retaliated by bombing military bases but deliberately avoided targets in densely populated areas.
NOTE: The legacy rendering is accurate. Self-review retained the support-verb construction because it is the idiomatic formal equivalent and separated it from personal revenge.
213 to colonize => ein Gebiet kolonisieren | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=kolonisierten
REG: Formal historical and political usage for bringing a territory under colonial rule and reorganizing it around the colonizing power's interests.
TRAP: Besiedeln means settling or populating a place and does not by itself express colonial domination; kolonisieren can denote settlement or imperial subjugation depending on context.
EX: Europäische Mächte kolonisierten große Teile Afrikas, zogen willkürliche Grenzen und ordneten lokale Wirtschaftsstrukturen ihren eigenen strategischen Interessen unter. || European powers colonized large parts of Africa, drew arbitrary borders and subordinated local economic structures to their own strategic interests.
NOTE: The legacy verb is accurate. Self-review fixed the imperial-rule sense in the example because the isolated English verb can also refer more narrowly to settlement.
215 to advocate => sich für etw. einsetzen | alts=für etw. eintreten | lexical-verb | CLOZE=setzten sich | für | ein
REG: Neutral-formal political and policy usage for actively supporting and promoting a proposal, principle or group.
TRAP: English advocate can take a direct object, but sich einsetzen is reflexive and governs für plus accusative; befürworten may express approval without active advocacy.
EX: Mehrere Ökonomen setzten sich für gezielte Wiederaufbauhilfen ein, weil pauschale Handelsbeschränkungen vor allem städtische Arbeiter und kleine Betriebe belasteten. || Several economists advocated targeted reconstruction aid because blanket trade restrictions placed the greatest burden on urban workers and small businesses.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back: befürworten is valid for endorsing something, while the retained frame captures sustained or active advocacy.
216 to penetrate => in etw. eindringen | alts=in etw. vordringen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=drangen | in | ein
REG: Neutral-formal security and intelligence usage for entering a protected territory, organization or system despite barriers.
TRAP: English penetrate takes a direct object, whereas eindringen is intransitive and governs in plus accusative for motion into a target; its perfect uses sein.
EX: Vom Ausland gesteuerte Agentennetzwerke drangen in zentrale Ministerien ein und verschafften sich über Jahre Zugang zu vertraulichen Verhandlungsunterlagen. || Foreign-controlled agent networks penetrated key ministries and gained access to confidential negotiating documents over a period of years.
NOTE: The isolated source is polysemous. This card selects covert institutional entry; self-review supplied the governed preposition omitted by the legacy infinitive.
217 to strategize => eine Strategie entwickeln | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=entwickelte eine Strategie
REG: Neutral-formal planning and policy construction for formulating a deliberate course of action toward a defined objective.
TRAP: English readily forms a verb from strategy; standard German usually uses eine Strategie entwickeln or strategisch planen rather than treating strategisieren as unmarked.
EX: Der Planungsstab entwickelte eine Strategie, um den Einfluss verbündeter Parteien auszubauen, ohne eine offene militärische Konfrontation mit der Gegenseite zu riskieren. || The planning staff devised a strategy to expand the influence of allied parties without risking an open military confrontation with the other side.
NOTE: Materially sharpens old_back: strategisch vorgehen means acting strategically, whereas this support-verb expression captures the prior work of formulating a strategy.
219 to delegate => etw. an jdn. delegieren | alts=etw. an jdn. übertragen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=delegierte | an
REG: Neutral-formal organizational and political usage for assigning a task or responsibility to a subordinate person or body.
TRAP: In the explicit productive frame, the delegated task is accusative and the recipient follows an plus accusative; delegieren can also mean sending someone as a representative.
EX: Die Parteiführung delegierte die Überwachung regionaler Medien an loyale Funktionäre, behielt jedoch die Kontrolle über Personalentscheidungen und Sanktionsmaßnahmen. || The party leadership delegated the monitoring of regional media to loyal officials but retained control over personnel decisions and sanctions.
NOTE: The legacy verb is accurate but omits the productive task-recipient frame. Self-review retained übertragen as an interchangeable less Latinate alternative in this context.
220 to intimidate => jdn. einschüchtern | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=schüchterten | ein
REG: Neutral-critical political and legal usage for deliberately frightening someone into silence, compliance or inaction.
TRAP: Einschüchtern is separable in finite clauses: schüchtert ... ein; bedrohen foregrounds an explicit threat, whereas einschüchtern foregrounds its intended coercive effect.
EX: Die Sicherheitskräfte schüchterten unabhängige Journalisten ein, indem sie Redaktionsräume durchsuchten, Angehörige verhörten und wiederholt mit dem Entzug von Lizenzen drohten. || The security forces intimidated independent journalists by searching newsrooms, questioning relatives and repeatedly threatening to revoke licenses.
NOTE: The lexical choice in old_back is sound but incorrectly capitalized as an infinitive. Self-review added the separable finite morphology and the contrast with making an explicit threat.
222 to undermine => etw. untergraben | alts=etw. aushöhlen | lexical-verb | CLOZE=untergruben
REG: Neutral-formal political and analytical usage for gradually weakening the authority, effectiveness or foundations of something.
TRAP: Unterminieren is valid but distinctly formal; unterwandern implies covert penetration from within, while untergraben can describe gradual erosion without infiltration.
EX: Die wiederholten Eingriffe in Gerichtsverfahren untergruben die institutionelle Unabhängigkeit und schwächten langfristig das Vertrauen der Bevölkerung in den Rechtsstaat. || Repeated interference in court proceedings undermined institutional independence and, over the long term, weakened public trust in the rule of law.
NOTE: Replaces old_back with the more idiomatic neutral-formal verb. Self-review preserved unterminieren as valid but excluded it from alts because its register is more marked.

