The shared triage is coherent: the 12 COLD_WAR_VOCAB-owned exact duplicates are retained in all five languages, while most cross-language keep/drop differences reflect real noncognates, government, or regional terminology. In the first language pass I’ve already found a definite repetition defect (`Die Unterhändler verhandelten`) and am tracking several examples whose loaded framing needs explicit attribution, plus the German MAD term for specialist verification.
exec
/bin/bash -lc ".venv/bin/python - <<'PY'
import json,re
p='idiomatic/grammar/data/exercises2/batches/output/es_cold_war_vocab_b01_notes.json'
for n in json.load(open(p)):
 t=' | '.join(re.findall(r'\\{\\{c1::(.*?)\\}\\}',n['cloze']))
 print(f\"{n['id'][-3:]} {n['en']} => {n['tl']} | alts={'; '.join(n['alts']) or '—'} | {n['category']} | CLOZE={t}\\nREG: {n['register']}\\nTRAP: {n['trap'] or '—'}\\nEX: {n['example_tl']} || {n['example_en']}\\nNOTE: {n['note'] or '—'}\")
PY" in /home/admin/projects/idiomatic
