001 the Military coup => le coup d’État militaire | alts=le putsch militaire | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le coup d’État militaire
REG: Neutral-formal historical and political term; putsch militaire is more vivid and often suggests a rapid seizure by a small armed group.
TRAP: Coup alone is too broad in French; the fixed political expression is coup d’État, with a capital É to denote the state as an institution.
EX: Le coup d’État militaire de 1973 a renversé le gouvernement élu, dissous le Parlement et installé durablement l’armée au cœur du pouvoir chilien. || The 1973 military coup overthrew the elected government, dissolved Parliament, and durably installed the armed forces at the heart of Chilean power.
NOTE: Old_back gives the correct established term. Self-review retained militaire because it identifies the agent rather than merely repeating the violent character of a coup.
006 the Guerillas => les guérilleros | alts=les combattants de la guérilla | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les guérilleros
REG: Standard historical and journalistic plural for individual guerrilla fighters; guérilla instead denotes the irregular force or form of warfare collectively.
TRAP: The legacy les guérillas changes people into organizations or campaigns; an individual fighter is un guérillero, feminine une guérillera.
EX: Les guérilleros contrôlaient les routes secondaires, mais dépendaient des villages voisins pour obtenir des renseignements, des vivres et des soins médicaux. || The guerrillas controlled the secondary roads but depended on neighboring villages for intelligence, provisions, and medical care.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which uses the collective noun guérilla in the plural rather than naming the fighters intended by the English prompt.
011 the Capitalism => le capitalisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le capitalisme
REG: Neutral term in history and political economy; surrounding prose determines whether it is descriptive, approving, or critical.
TRAP: Capitalisme names the economic system, while capitaliste can be an adjective or a person; French common names of doctrines remain lowercase.
EX: Le capitalisme a survécu à la guerre froide en se transformant, sans pour autant réduire les inégalités que ses défenseurs promettaient d’atténuer. || Capitalism survived the Cold War by transforming itself, without thereby reducing the inequalities its defenders promised to alleviate.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB. Old_back is accurate.
014 the Regime => le régime | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le régime
REG: Neutral-formal political term for a system or form of government, though an unmodified reference to a particular régime can sound critical.
TRAP: French régime is broader than dictatorship and can describe a democratic constitutional order; the accent also distinguishes it from English spelling.
EX: Le régime autorisait plusieurs partis, mais contrôlait leur financement, limitait leur accès aux médias et invalidait les candidatures jugées menaçantes. || The regime allowed several parties but controlled their funding, limited their access to the media, and disqualified candidacies deemed threatening.
NOTE: Old_back supplies the correct headword. Self-review replaced annulait les candidatures with idiomatic invalidait and avoided falsely defining every régime as a dictatorship.
016 Authoritarian => autoritaire | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=autoritaire
REG: Neutral-formal political adjective for power that demands obedience and sharply limits pluralism or institutional accountability.
TRAP: The legacy l’autoritarisme is the noun authoritarianism, not the requested adjective; autoritaire can also describe a domineering person outside politics.
EX: Le gouvernement demeurait autoritaire malgré les élections, car l’opposition restait exclue des médias et les magistrats subissaient des pressions systématiques. || The government remained authoritarian despite the elections because the opposition was excluded from the media and judges faced systematic pressure.
NOTE: Retained here under the exact-duplicate report. Materially corrects old_back’s part-of-speech error and restores lowercase for an isolated adjective.
018 the Exile => l’exil | alts=l’exil politique | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’exil
REG: Neutral-formal historical noun for forced or constrained residence outside one’s country; exil politique makes the cause explicit.
TRAP: Exil names the condition or displacement, whereas exilé names a person; the fixed phrase en exil normally has no article.
EX: L’exil a permis à certains opposants de poursuivre leur combat politique, tout en les éloignant des réseaux clandestins restés dans leur pays. || Exile enabled some opponents to continue their political struggle while distancing them from the clandestine networks that remained in their country.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary initial capitalization; the card supplies the productive exil/exilé and en exil distinctions.
020 the Junta => la junte militaire | alts=la junte | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La junte militaire
REG: Standard historical and journalistic term, usually critical, for a military leadership that has collectively seized political power.
TRAP: Unlike Spanish junta or Portuguese junta, French junte is not the ordinary word for a committee or board; it strongly evokes authoritarian rule.
EX: La junte militaire a suspendu la Constitution, interdit les syndicats et confié les principaux ministères à des officiers issus de l’état-major. || The military junta suspended the Constitution, banned trade unions, and entrusted the main ministries to officers from the general staff.
NOTE: Sharpens old_back by making the military scope explicit in the production headword; the shorter legacy form remains a valid contextual alternative.
021 the Counterinsurgency => la contre-insurrection | alts=la lutte anti-insurrectionnelle | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La contre-insurrection
REG: Specialist military and security-policy term for the combined political, intelligence, policing, and military response to an insurgency.
TRAP: Contre-insurrection is broader than a single counterattack: it can include intelligence, propaganda, population control, development programs, and coercion.
EX: La contre-insurrection combinait opérations militaires, renseignement et contrôle des populations, au risque de confondre soutien civil et participation armée. || Counterinsurgency combined military operations, intelligence, and population control, at the risk of conflating civilian support with armed participation.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate; the example fixes the doctrine’s integrated scope rather than reducing it to combat against insurgents.
022 the OAS (Organization of American States) => l’OEA (Organisation des États américains) | alts=l’Organisation des États américains | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’OEA
REG: Official institutional name and French initialism; OEA is standard in diplomatic, historical, and journalistic prose.
TRAP: Do not carry over English OAS: French reorders the initials to OEA, matching Organisation des États américains.
EX: L’OEA a condamné officiellement le coup d’État, mais ses divisions internes ont empêché l’adoption rapide de mesures collectives contraignantes. || The OAS officially condemned the coup, but its internal divisions prevented the swift adoption of binding collective measures.
NOTE: Old_back correctly localizes both the official name and its acronym. The cloze tests the acronym actually used in the contextual sentence.
023 the Proxy wars => les guerres par procuration | alts=les conflits par procuration | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les guerres par procuration
REG: Established international-relations term for conflicts in which outside powers support local belligerents instead of fighting one another directly.
TRAP: The fixed phrase is par procuration, not a literal guerre de procuration; it foregrounds outside sponsorship without erasing local actors’ own aims.
EX: Les guerres par procuration ont permis aux superpuissances de s’opposer sans s’affronter directement, en transférant aux sociétés locales l’essentiel des destructions. || Proxy wars allowed the superpowers to oppose one another without fighting directly, transferring most of the destruction to local societies.
NOTE: Old_back gives the established French term. Self-review removed a lexical contradiction around confrontation and preserved local societies’ agency.
026 the Diplomacy => la diplomatie | alts=l’action diplomatique | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La diplomatie
REG: Neutral-formal term for the conduct of relations and negotiation between political entities; it can denote both a practice and an institutional field.
TRAP: Diplomatie is the practice or field, diplomate is a person, and diplomatique is the adjective; these forms are not interchangeable.
EX: La diplomatie a maintenu des canaux discrets entre les blocs, même lorsque les déclarations publiques excluaient tout compromis sur les missiles. || Diplomacy maintained discreet channels between the blocs even when public statements ruled out any compromise over the missiles.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB. Old_back is accurate.
029 the Trade barriers => les obstacles au commerce | alts=les entraves aux échanges; les barrières commerciales | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les obstacles au commerce
REG: Formal trade-policy term covering tariffs and non-tariff measures; barrières commerciales is common, while obstacles au commerce is especially clear institutionally.
TRAP: The concept is broader than tariffs: quotas, licensing, discriminatory standards, and administrative procedures can also constitute obstacles au commerce.
EX: Les obstacles au commerce ont protégé certaines industries nationales, mais également accru le coût des équipements importés dont dépendait la modernisation économique. || Trade barriers protected certain domestic industries but also increased the cost of imported equipment on which economic modernization depended.
NOTE: Reorders old_back into the clearer institutional collocation obstacles au commerce. Self-review replaced a debatable transitive use of renchérir with accru le coût.
030 the Censorship => la censure | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La censure
REG: Neutral-formal media, political, and legal term for suppressing or prohibiting information, expression, or publication.
TRAP: English censorship is French censure, but English censure meaning formal condemnation is blâme or condamnation; the apparent cognate reverses the learner’s expectation.
EX: La censure empêchait les journaux de faire état des arrestations arbitraires, tandis que la radio officielle attribuait toute contestation à des agents étrangers. || Censorship prevented newspapers from reporting arbitrary arrests, while official radio attributed all dissent to foreign agents.
NOTE: Retained here under the exact-duplicate report. Self-review replaced translation-like publier les arrestations with idiomatic faire état des arrestations; old_back incorrectly capitalizes the noun.
032 the Neoliberalism => le néolibéralisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le néolibéralisme
REG: Established but contested term in political economy; careful prose should specify the policies, institutions, or historical project being described.
TRAP: French libéralisme commonly foregrounds market economics, unlike liberalism in contemporary U.S. politics; néolibéralisme is not simply a newer form of social liberalism.
EX: Le néolibéralisme a redéfini le rôle de l’État sans le réduire, en mobilisant ses institutions pour ouvrir des secteurs publics à la concurrence. || Neoliberalism redefined the role of the state without reducing it, mobilizing its institutions to open public sectors to competition.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB. Old_back is accurate.
035 the Containment => la politique d’endiguement | alts=l’endiguement | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La politique d’endiguement
REG: Established Cold War history and international-relations term for the U.S. strategy of limiting further Soviet expansion rather than overthrowing the Soviet state.
TRAP: In this doctrine, containment is endiguement, not confinement or contention; politique d’endiguement makes the geopolitical sense explicit.
EX: La politique d’endiguement visait moins à renverser l’Union soviétique qu’à freiner l’expansion de son influence par des alliances et des aides ciblées. || The policy of containment aimed less to overthrow the Soviet Union than to curb the expansion of its influence through alliances and targeted aid.
NOTE: Sharpens old_back by promoting the full doctrinal label politique d’endiguement; the shorter endiguement remains natural when Cold War context is already explicit.
037 the Pro-U.S. factions => les factions proaméricaines | alts=les factions favorables aux États-Unis | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les factions proaméricaines
REG: Neutral-formal geopolitical label for groups aligned with or supportive of U.S. policy; it does not necessarily imply direct control by Washington.
TRAP: The prefix pro- expresses support or alignment; in the standard closed spelling proaméricain, the adjective agrees normally: factions proaméricaines.
EX: Les factions proaméricaines recevaient des armes et un soutien financier, sans pour autant partager l’ensemble des priorités stratégiques de Washington. || The pro-U.S. factions received weapons and financial support without necessarily sharing all of Washington’s strategic priorities.
NOTE: Standardizes legacy pro-américaines to the closed dictionary spelling proaméricaines. Self-review states alignment without inferring that the factions were mere proxies.
039 Disappeared => les disparus | alts=les victimes de disparition forcée | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les disparus
REG: Historical and human-rights plural for people abducted or detained whose fate and whereabouts are deliberately concealed by state agents or their allies.
TRAP: A disparu can simply be a missing person; the Cold War term les disparus invokes enforced disappearance, and the legacy feminine adjective lacks the required article.
EX: Les disparus figuraient rarement dans les registres officiels, tandis que leurs familles cherchaient une trace vérifiable dans les prisons, les casernes et les archives. || The disappeared rarely appeared in official registers, while their families searched for a verifiable trace in prisons, barracks, and archives.
NOTE: The Cold War context supports the substantivized plural the disappeared, materially correcting old_back. Self-review replaced the literary bare-noun list with natural prepositional phrasing.
040 the Economic sanctions => les sanctions économiques | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les sanctions économiques
REG: Neutral-formal international-relations term for coercive restrictions on trade, finance, assets, or transactions imposed to change an actor’s conduct.
TRAP: French sanctionner can exceptionally mean to ratify or approve in formal institutional usage; sanctions économiques unambiguously denotes coercive penalties.
EX: Les sanctions économiques ont réduit les recettes d’exportation du régime, mais leurs effets ont surtout pesé sur les ménages privés de médicaments importés. || Economic sanctions reduced the regime’s export revenue, but their effects fell mainly on households deprived of imported medicines.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary capitalization. The card records the narrower coercive sense selected by the international-relations context.
### fr b02
041 the Resistance => la Résistance | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La Résistance
REG: Established historical proper name for the French resistance movements of the Second World War; lowercase résistance is the generic common noun.
TRAP: Capitalization carries meaning: la Résistance denotes the historical movements, whereas la résistance can describe opposition to any occupier, regime, or policy.
EX: La Résistance a survécu dans la mémoire politique d’après-guerre comme un héritage disputé, mobilisé aussi bien par les gaullistes que par les communistes. || The Resistance endured in postwar political memory as a contested legacy, invoked by Gaullists and Communists alike.
NOTE: Old_back has the right term and capitalization. Self-review fixed the example to the specifically French historical sense signaled by the source capital.
044 the Ideology => l’idéologie | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’idéologie
REG: Neutral academic and political noun; it can become critical when it implies a rigid or distorting system of ideas.
TRAP: French idéologie is not automatically pejorative, and common names of doctrines and schools of thought normally remain lowercase.
EX: L’idéologie structurait les alliances bien au-delà des intérêts immédiats, en imposant aux gouvernements une lecture binaire de chaque crise régionale. || Ideology structured alliances far beyond immediate interests by imposing a binary reading of every regional crisis on governments.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB; old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary capitalization.
045 the Arms race => la course aux armements | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La course aux armements
REG: Established neutral-formal term in strategic studies and modern history, applicable to both nuclear and conventional weapons competition.
TRAP: The fixed complement is aux armements; neither a literal course des armes nor the narrower course aux armes is idiomatic for this geopolitical concept.
EX: La course aux armements a absorbé des ressources considérables, tout en alimentant une insécurité que chaque nouveau missile prétendait précisément réduire. || The arms race absorbed considerable resources while fueling an insecurity that each new missile purported precisely to reduce.
NOTE: Old_back gives the established expression. Self-review removed a looser singular variant because it is less canonical and not freely interchangeable.
046 Interventionism => l’interventionnisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’interventionnisme
REG: Political-science term, often critical; context must show whether it concerns state action in the economy or intervention in another country.
TRAP: French interventionnisme has two consecutive n letters and names a doctrine or systematic policy, not merely one intervention ponctuelle.
EX: L’interventionnisme de Washington en Amérique latine associait aide économique, pression diplomatique et opérations clandestines afin de contenir les gouvernements jugés hostiles. || Washington’s interventionism in Latin America combined economic aid, diplomatic pressure, and covert operations to contain governments deemed hostile.
NOTE: Old_back is correct. Self-review made the foreign-policy sense explicit because the same French noun also denotes domestic economic interventionism.
051 the Christian Democracy => la Démocratie chrétienne | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La Démocratie chrétienne
REG: Historical party label in the Chilean context; lowercase la démocratie chrétienne instead names the broader political tradition.
TRAP: Do not transfer Italian title capitalization mechanically: the generic current is la démocratie chrétienne, while the Chilean party is also called le Parti démocrate-chrétien.
EX: La Démocratie chrétienne chilienne a porté un projet réformiste qui modernisait l’agriculture sans remettre entièrement en cause les structures de propriété. || Chilean Christian Democracy pursued a reformist project that modernized agriculture without entirely challenging property structures.
NOTE: Interprets the capitalized source within the surrounding Chilean sequence as a party label; the generic tradition is la démocratie chrétienne. Self-review removed a gender-changing alternative and replaced a contestable junta claim with the party’s documented agrarian-policy context.
052 the Land redistribution => la redistribution des terres | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La redistribution des terres
REG: Neutral-formal term in agrarian history and development policy, specifically referring to the transfer or reallocation of landholdings.
TRAP: Redistribution des terres is narrower than réforme agraire, which can also encompass credit, tenure rules, cooperatives, and rural infrastructure.
EX: La redistribution des terres promise par le gouvernement menaçait les grands propriétaires, mais son application inégale déçut rapidement les communautés rurales. || The land redistribution promised by the government threatened large landowners, but its uneven implementation quickly disappointed rural communities.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained this item alongside agrarian reform because it denotes the narrower transfer mechanism, not the whole reform program.
054 the Expropriation => l’expropriation | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’expropriation
REG: Neutral-formal legal and political-economy term for a compulsory taking; historical accounts may use it without endorsing the measure’s legality.
TRAP: In legal French, expropriation normally invokes public purpose and compensation; confiscation denotes a punitive taking and is not a neutral synonym.
EX: L’expropriation des compagnies minières devait transférer leurs actifs à l’État, sous réserve d’une indemnisation dont le calcul provoqua un conflit diplomatique. || The expropriation of the mining companies was to transfer their assets to the state, subject to compensation whose calculation caused a diplomatic conflict.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review kept the legal distinction from confiscation explicit rather than treating the two as stylistic alternatives.
057 the Peasants => les paysans | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les paysans
REG: Standard in social and agrarian history; in contemporary occupational reporting, agriculteurs is usually more neutral and institutionally precise.
TRAP: Paysan need not carry the contemptuous force of English peasant, but it frames people as a rural social group rather than simply as farm operators.
EX: Les paysans se sont mobilisés lorsque la réforme agraire a cessé d’être une promesse abstraite pour modifier concrètement la répartition des terres. || The peasants mobilized when agrarian reform ceased to be an abstract promise and began concretely changing the distribution of land.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review rejected agriculteurs because it loses the historical class framing, then replaced rejoindre les mobilisations with the more idiomatic se mobiliser.
061 the Militancy => le militantisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le militantisme
REG: Neutral term for sustained activist or party engagement; the surrounding noun or adjective normally identifies the cause or organization.
TRAP: English militancy often suggests aggressiveness, whereas French militantisme can describe peaceful activism; combativité is preferable when confrontation itself is central.
EX: Le militantisme syndical s’est intensifié dans les mines, où les revendications salariales se mêlaient désormais à une contestation plus large du régime. || Trade-union activism intensified in the mines, where wage demands were now merging with a broader challenge to the regime.
NOTE: Old_back gives the intended French term, but the English prompt is potentially stronger; the example fixes the organized-activism sense inherited from the source sequence.
063 the Agrarian reform => la réforme agraire | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La réforme agraire
REG: Established neutral-formal term in rural history and development policy, broader than the redistribution of land alone.
TRAP: English agrarian corresponds here to French agraire, not agrarien, which chiefly describes an ideology or a partisan of agrarian interests.
EX: La réforme agraire visait non seulement à morceler les grands domaines, mais aussi à élargir l’accès au crédit et aux infrastructures rurales. || Agrarian reform aimed not only to break up the large estates but also to broaden access to credit and rural infrastructure.
NOTE: Kept as the canonical singular specialist term; the plural land reforms item 043 was dropped as a constructional duplicate.
066 the Trade unions => les syndicats | alts=les organisations syndicales | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les syndicats
REG: Standard neutral plural in labor, political, and journalistic contexts; organisations syndicales is a slightly more institutional alternative.
TRAP: English trade union is syndicat, not union commerciale; French union more often names a union or federation explicitly bearing that word in its title.
EX: Les syndicats ont coordonné une grève nationale malgré l’état d’urgence, transformant des revendications professionnelles en épreuve politique pour le gouvernement. || The trade unions coordinated a nationwide strike despite the state of emergency, turning workplace demands into a political test for the government.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review limited the alternative to a genuinely interchangeable institutional phrase rather than adding the narrower syndicats de salariés.
069 the Inequality => les inégalités | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les inégalités
REG: Standard policy and social-science usage; the plural normally denotes measurable disparities, while singular l’inégalité is more abstract or theoretical.
TRAP: Do not preserve English singular mechanically: French policy prose usually discusses les inégalités when referring to gaps in income, status, or access.
EX: Les inégalités se sont creusées malgré la croissance, car les gains de productivité ont surtout profité aux groupes déjà proches du pouvoir. || Inequalities widened despite growth because productivity gains mainly benefited groups that were already close to power.
NOTE: Old_back’s plural is idiomatic for the policy sense. Self-review retained it rather than forcing source-number symmetry with the more abstract l’inégalité.
070 Left-wing => de gauche | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=de gauche
REG: Neutral, invariable adjective phrase used after a noun to describe political alignment; suitable in both journalism and academic prose.
TRAP: The legacy Gauche is a capitalized noun, not the requested adjective; ordinary ideological uses of de gauche take no capital letter.
EX: Les partis de gauche défendaient la nationalisation du cuivre, mais divergeaient profondément sur le rythme des réformes et le recours à la mobilisation populaire. || Left-wing parties supported nationalizing copper but differed profoundly over the pace of reforms and recourse to popular mobilization.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which supplies the noun gauche and changes the source’s part of speech.
073 the Protest => la contestation | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La contestation
REG: Neutral political and journalistic term for sustained collective challenge; protestation more often denotes a discrete objection or public statement.
TRAP: French manifestation is a street demonstration, not the whole phenomenon of protest; contestation can also include strikes, occupations, publications, and boycotts.
EX: La contestation étudiante a débordé les campus lorsque la police a interdit les rassemblements et que les journaux ont minimisé les violences. || Student protest spread beyond the campuses when police banned gatherings and newspapers played down the violence.
NOTE: Diverges from old_back: contestation better captures sustained political protest, whereas protestation remains possible for a discrete objection or public statement.
074 the Dissent => la dissidence | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La dissidence
REG: Formal political and intellectual term, especially for opposition expressed under a dominant doctrine or authoritarian system.
TRAP: English dissent can be an ordinary désaccord; French dissidence implies a more consequential break with an authority, party line, or governing orthodoxy.
EX: La dissidence intellectuelle s’exprimait dans des revues clandestines, dont chaque exemplaire franchissait les frontières au prix de risques considérables pour les rédacteurs. || Intellectual dissent found expression in clandestine journals, each copy of which crossed borders at considerable risk to the editors.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review kept the stronger political sense and replaced circulait par des revues with the idiomatic s’exprimait dans des revues.
077 the Popular unity => l’Unité populaire | alts=l’Unidad Popular | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’Unité populaire
REG: Proper historical name of the Chilean left-wing coalition associated with Salvador Allende; the original Spanish name also occurs in French scholarship.
TRAP: Capitalize Unité when naming the coalition; lowercase unité populaire is merely a generic phrase meaning popular unity.
EX: L’Unité populaire rassemblait socialistes, communistes et autres forces de gauche autour d’un programme de transformations institutionnelles porté par Salvador Allende. || Popular Unity brought together Socialists, Communists, and other left-wing forces around a program of institutional transformations championed by Salvador Allende.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back by restoring the historical coalition’s capitalization; self-review replaced the less idiomatic programme conduit with programme porté.
078 the Divestment => le désinvestissement | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le désinvestissement
REG: Formal economics and finance term for reducing investment or disposing of assets; retrait des capitaux is more explicit in a cross-border context.
TRAP: Désengagement is broader and can be political or military, while cession names a sale; neither automatically conveys the reduction of invested capital.
EX: Le désinvestissement étranger s’est accéléré après les nationalisations, privant le pays de capitaux au moment même où ses besoins industriels augmentaient. || Foreign divestment accelerated after the nationalizations, depriving the country of capital just as its industrial needs were increasing.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review excluded désengagement because it is broader than the financial sense and removed retrait des capitaux because clean substitution would require moving the example’s adjective.
080 the Foreign relations => les relations extérieures | alts=les relations internationales | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les relations extérieures
REG: Neutral-formal institutional term for a government’s external relationships; relations internationales is broader and also names the academic field.
TRAP: Avoid the English calque relations étrangères; French normally uses relations extérieures for an institution’s external affairs or relations internationales more broadly.
EX: Les relations extérieures du gouvernement se sont dégradées après l’expropriation des entreprises américaines et le rapprochement diplomatique avec Cuba. || The government’s foreign relations deteriorated after the expropriation of American companies and the diplomatic rapprochement with Cuba.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained relations internationales because it remains idiomatic in the example, while noting its broader possible scope.

