084 Developmentalism => le développementalisme | alts=le développementisme | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le développementalisme
REG: Specialist economics and political-science term for a state-led development doctrine; développementisme is a less common but established variant.
TRAP: Développementalisme names a doctrine or policy model, not development itself; le développement and l’aide au développement are therefore not synonyms.
EX: Le développementalisme brésilien associait planification étatique, protection du marché intérieur et industrialisation rapide, au nom d’une autonomie économique encore largement inachevée. || Brazilian developmentalism combined state planning, protection of the domestic market, and rapid industrialization in the name of an economic autonomy that remained largely unrealized.
NOTE: Old_back gives the accepted headword. Self-review retained développementisme only as an established variant and made the doctrine, rather than generic development, explicit.
085 the Import substitution => la substitution aux importations | alts=l’industrialisation par substitution aux importations | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La substitution aux importations
REG: Specialist development-economics term; the longer alternative explicitly names the industrialization strategy built around the policy.
TRAP: The established complement is aux importations, not the legacy des importations; the policy replaces imported goods with domestic production rather than merely restricting imports.
EX: La substitution aux importations devait réduire la dépendance extérieure du Brésil, mais elle exigeait des équipements et des technologies encore achetés à l’étranger. || Import substitution was intended to reduce Brazil’s external dependence, but it required equipment and technologies that were still purchased abroad.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back’s nonstandard substitution des importations. Self-review checked that the longer alternative remains grammatical and equivalent in the example.
087 Tropicalia (referring to the music and art movement) => le mouvement Tropicália | alts=le tropicalisme | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le mouvement Tropicália
REG: Established cultural-history name for the Brazilian movement of the late 1960s; tropicalisme is the usual French descriptive alternative.
TRAP: Preserve the Portuguese accent in Tropicália; tropical names a climate or style and does not by itself name this Brazilian movement.
EX: Le mouvement Tropicália a mêlé avant-garde, culture populaire et guitares électriques, transformant l’expérimentation musicale en critique indirecte de la dictature brésilienne. || The Tropicália movement blended the avant-garde, popular culture, and electric guitars, turning musical experimentation into an indirect critique of Brazil’s dictatorship.
NOTE: Old_back explains the referent but does not supply a clean headword. Self-review used mouvement Tropicália to avoid uncertain article use before the bare proper name.
088 Clandestine => clandestin, clandestine | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=clandestins
REG: Neutral-formal adjective for activity deliberately concealed because it is prohibited or dangerous; frequent in political and resistance history.
TRAP: Old_back changes the adjective into the abstract noun clandestinité; French adjectives must agree, as in un réseau clandestin and une revue clandestine.
EX: Des journaux clandestins circulaient entre les universités malgré la censure, reliant les opposants exilés aux réseaux de résistance restés dans le pays. || Clandestine newspapers circulated among the universities despite censorship, linking exiled opponents with resistance networks that remained inside the country.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which supplies a noun and therefore changes the source’s part of speech.
090 the Industrialists => les industriels | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=industriels
REG: Neutral journalistic and economic plural for owners or senior figures in industry; context normally identifies the sector or political role.
TRAP: Les industriels denotes industrial business leaders or firms in this sense, not industrial workers, who are les ouvriers de l’industrie or les travailleurs de l’industrie.
EX: Une partie des industriels a soutenu le coup d’État lorsque les réformes sociales lui ont paru menacer ses investissements et son influence sur le gouvernement. || Some industrialists supported the coup when the social reforms appeared to threaten their investments and their influence over the government.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained the business-leader sense but narrowed the example from all industrialists to une partie des industriels to avoid an overbroad historical claim.
091 the Military governance => le régime militaire | alts=le pouvoir militaire | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le régime militaire
REG: Neutral-formal political and historical term for rule by the armed forces; pouvoir militaire emphasizes the authority they exercise.
TRAP: Gouvernance militaire is an awkward English calque for military rule and may instead suggest administrative methods; gouvernement militaire more narrowly names the executive.
EX: Le régime militaire a centralisé les décisions économiques tout en confiant leur mise en œuvre à une technocratie soucieuse de rassurer les investisseurs étrangers. || The military regime centralized economic decisions while entrusting their implementation to a technocracy eager to reassure foreign investors.
NOTE: Materially diverges from old_back: the surrounding Cold War sequence selects military rule, for which régime militaire is more idiomatic than gouvernance militaire.
093 the Amazon => l’Amazonie | alts=la région amazonienne | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’Amazonie
REG: Standard geographical name for the Amazon region or basin; the descriptive alternative is useful when territorial scope needs emphasis.
TRAP: French distinguishes l’Amazonie, the region, from l’Amazone, the river; the Brazilian development context selects the former.
EX: L’Amazonie est devenue un front d’expansion économique où routes, mines et élevage ont accéléré la déforestation au nom de l’intégration nationale. || The Amazon became a frontier of economic expansion where roads, mines, and cattle farming accelerated deforestation in the name of national integration.
NOTE: Old_back correctly resolves the English ambiguity in favor of the region. Self-review retained an alternative that preserves that regional sense.
095 Cinema Novo => le Cinema Novo | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le Cinema Novo
REG: Established proper name of the Brazilian film movement that emerged around the turn of the 1960s; standard in film criticism and cultural history.
TRAP: Keep the Portuguese movement name untranslated and without a French accent on Cinema; nouveau cinéma or Nouvelle Vague would name different concepts.
EX: Le Cinema Novo a placé la pauvreté et les rapports de pouvoir au centre d’une esthétique cinématographique affranchie des modèles commerciaux dominants. || Cinema Novo placed poverty and power relations at the center of a cinematic aesthetic freed from dominant commercial models.
NOTE: Old_back adds a French common-noun article and capitalization that obscure the preserved Portuguese proper name. Self-review follows established French film usage, le Cinema Novo.
096 the Megacity => la mégapole | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=mégapole
REG: Neutral term in urban geography and policy for a city of exceptionally large population; exact thresholds depend on the analytical source.
TRAP: In precise geographical usage, mégalopole more often denotes a vast urbanized network comprising several metropolitan areas, not simply one megacity.
EX: São Paulo est devenue une mégapole dont l’expansion périphérique a dépassé les capacités de transport, de logement et d’assainissement des pouvoirs publics. || São Paulo became a megacity whose peripheral expansion outstripped the public authorities’ transport, housing, and sanitation capacity.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review avoided claiming a universal population threshold and retained the item for the useful mégapole versus mégalopole distinction.
097 Autarky => l’autarcie | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’autarcie
REG: Formal economics and political-history term for national economic self-sufficiency, whether pursued as a condition or a doctrine.
TRAP: Do not transfer Italian autarchia as *autarchie*: French has autarcie with c; autocratie instead concerns the concentration of political power.
EX: L’autarcie promise par le régime a surtout renchéri les biens d’équipement, sans supprimer la dépendance aux technologies et aux crédits étrangers. || The autarky promised by the regime mainly made capital goods more expensive, without eliminating dependence on foreign technologies and credit.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review replaced a generic cognate warning with the concrete Italian spelling interference and the autocratie distinction.
098 the Dependency theory => la théorie de la dépendance | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La théorie de la dépendance
REG: Established specialist term in development economics, sociology, and Latin American intellectual history.
TRAP: The fixed term contains the definite article de la; it analyzes structural center–periphery relations, not an individual state of personal dépendance.
EX: La théorie de la dépendance expliquait le sous-développement non comme un retard passager, mais comme le produit durable de relations économiques asymétriques. || Dependency theory explained underdevelopment not as a temporary lag but as the lasting product of asymmetrical economic relations.
NOTE: Old_back gives the established term. Self-review made its structural analytical sense explicit rather than treating the phrase as merely compositional.
100 the Favelas => les favelas | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les favelas
REG: Standard Brazilian social-geography term in French; the singular is feminine, une favela, and the common noun is lowercase.
TRAP: Favela is not an exact synonym for every bidonville or quartier pauvre; it names a historically and institutionally specific Brazilian urban form.
EX: Les favelas ne forment pas un ensemble homogène : leur histoire, leur accès aux services publics et leurs relations avec l’État varient fortement. || Favelas do not form a homogeneous whole: their history, access to public services, and relations with the state vary greatly.
NOTE: Old_back has the right loanword but unnecessary capitalization. Self-review used the plural requested by the source while stating the otherwise hidden feminine gender.
101 Bureaucratic-authoritarian => bureaucratique-autoritaire | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=bureaucratique-autoritaire
REG: Specialist political-science adjective, especially associated with analyses of Latin American states and regimes under military rule.
TRAP: The compound is lowercase and hyphenated; both adjectival elements agree in the plural, as in des États bureaucratiques-autoritaires.
EX: L’État bureaucratique-autoritaire a exclu les classes populaires de la décision politique tout en confiant la stratégie économique à des experts civils et militaires. || The bureaucratic-authoritarian state excluded the popular classes from political decision-making while entrusting economic strategy to civilian and military experts.
NOTE: Old_back supplies the right specialist compound but incorrectly capitalizes an adjective. Self-review verified the established hyphenated political-science form.
104 the Caudillo => le caudillo | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le caudillo
REG: Historical and political term for a personalist leader in Spain or Latin America; often critical in modern analytical prose.
TRAP: Lowercase caudillo is the generic noun and is narrower than dictateur; uppercase Caudillo specifically served as Franco’s title.
EX: Le caudillo consolidait son autorité grâce à des réseaux de fidélité personnelle, distribuant charges et ressources en échange d’un soutien qui contournait les institutions. || The caudillo consolidated his authority through networks of personal loyalty, distributing offices and resources in exchange for support that bypassed institutions.
NOTE: Old_back has unnecessary capitalization for the generic Latin American sense. Self-review excluded dictateur and replaced par des fidélités with the idiomatic réseaux de fidélité personnelle.
105 Corporatism => le corporatisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le corporatisme
REG: Specialist political and institutional term; often historical or critical when describing state-managed representation of organized interests.
TRAP: Corporatisme is not the rule of commercial corporations: it organizes occupational interests into bodies recognized or controlled by the state.
EX: Le corporatisme regroupait patrons et salariés au sein d’organisations reconnues par l’État, afin de canaliser les conflits sociaux sans véritable pluralisme syndical. || Corporatism brought employers and employees together within state-recognized organizations in order to channel social conflicts without genuine trade-union pluralism.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary capitalization. Self-review sharpened the commercial-corporation distinction and replaced the calqued organisait en groupes with regroupait au sein d’organisations.
108 the Surveillance => la surveillance | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La surveillance
REG: Neutral-formal noun whose Cold War context selects systematic monitoring by police or intelligence services rather than benign supervision.
TRAP: French surveillance is broader than English surveillance and can mean ordinary supervision; administrative oversight may instead require contrôle or supervision depending on context.
EX: La surveillance des universitaires s’est étendue aux appels téléphoniques et au courrier, transformant toute critique du régime en indice potentiel de subversion. || Surveillance of university academics extended to telephone calls and mail, turning any criticism of the regime into potential evidence of subversion.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB; old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary capitalization.
110 Bossa Nova => la bossa-nova | alts=la bossanova | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La bossa-nova
REG: Standard feminine genre name; bossa-nova is the traditional dictionary spelling, while the joined 1990 spelling bossanova is also accepted.
TRAP: Unlike Portuguese bossa nova, the traditional French spelling uses a hyphen; as a genre name it is feminine and normally lowercase.
EX: La bossa-nova a projeté une image raffinée du Brésil urbain, tandis que ses harmonies circulaient bien au-delà des publics lusophones. || Bossa nova projected a sophisticated image of urban Brazil, while its harmonies circulated far beyond Portuguese-speaking audiences.
NOTE: Materially normalizes old_back’s capitalization and spacing. Self-review retained bossanova as the officially accepted rectified spelling and verified substitution in the example.
111 the Economic miracle => le miracle économique | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=miracle économique
REG: Established historical and journalistic label for rapid growth; often qualified or placed in quotation marks to signal its contested, selective framing.
TRAP: Miracle économique is an evaluative label, not a neutral statistical category; context should reveal whose gains, costs, and time frame are being described.
EX: Le prétendu miracle économique a affiché une croissance spectaculaire, mais cette performance a surtout enrichi les élites tout en reposant sur un endettement extérieur croissant. || The so-called economic miracle posted spectacular growth, but that performance mainly enriched the elites while relying on rising external debt.
