114 the Geopolitics => la géopolitique | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La géopolitique
REG: Neutral academic and journalistic singular denoting either the field or the power logic associated with a place, issue, or period.
TRAP: French normally uses singular géopolitique where awkward English may show geopolitics; géostratégie is narrower and is not a general synonym.
EX: La géopolitique de la guerre froide a souvent interprété les crises locales à travers le seul prisme de l’affrontement mondial entre les deux blocs. || Cold War geopolitics often interpreted local crises solely through the lens of the global confrontation between the two blocs.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB; self-review replaced a literal réduire à leur position with the idiomatic à travers le prisme de.
115 the Infrastructure => les infrastructures | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les infrastructures
REG: Standard policy and development usage for roads, networks, and public facilities; singular l’infrastructure is more collective or conceptual.
TRAP: Do not preserve English collective singular automatically: French commonly uses les infrastructures when several physical networks or facilities are meant.
EX: Les infrastructures construites par le régime ont désenclavé de nouveaux territoires, mais elles ont aussi facilité l’exploitation intensive de ressources jusque-là difficiles d’accès. || The infrastructure built by the regime opened up new territories, but it also facilitated the intensive exploitation of previously hard-to-reach resources.
NOTE: Retained in COLD_WAR_VOCAB under the exact-duplicate report rather than in FANCY_VOCAB. Self-review changed the singular to the idiomatic policy plural and replaced the calqued intégrer des territoires with désenclaver.
116 Mestizo => métis, métisse | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=métisse
REG: Historical and demographic adjective or person label; its racializing history requires precise context and attention to people’s own identifications.
TRAP: Métissage names mixing as a process or condition, not a mestizo person or the adjective métis; gender is visible in the feminine form métisse.
EX: Le recensement a longtemps figé une population métisse dans des catégories raciales qui masquaient la diversité des trajectoires familiales et des appartenances revendiquées. || The census long confined a mixed-race population within racial categories that obscured the diversity of family histories and claimed identities.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which gives the process noun métissage. The source is ambiguous between noun and adjective; this card teaches the adjective while the register line records the person-label use.
118 Afro-Brazilian => afro-brésilien, afro-brésilienne | alts=— | lexical-adjective | CLOZE=afro-brésiliennes
REG: Neutral adjective in cultural, historical, and demographic writing; the specific noun it modifies should make the intended cultural or population reference clear.
TRAP: The prefix afro- is invariable, while brésilien agrees with the noun: cultures afro-brésiliennes; the ordinary adjective is lowercase and hyphenated.
EX: Les religions afro-brésiliennes ont préservé des cosmologies et des pratiques communautaires malgré la répression policière, les préjugés sociaux et les tentatives d’assimilation. || Afro-Brazilian religions preserved cosmologies and communal practices despite police repression, social prejudice, and attempts at assimilation.
NOTE: Old_back gives only the masculine singular and incorrectly capitalizes the adjective. Self-review made agreement explicit and tested the feminine plural in context.
119 the Indigenous rights => les droits des peuples autochtones | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les droits des peuples autochtones
REG: Current neutral-formal institutional formula in international law and rights discourse, emphasizing peoples as collective rights holders.
TRAP: Indigène remains possible in some historical or scientific contexts, but droits des indigènes can sound reductive; autochtone is preferred in current institutional usage.
EX: Les droits des peuples autochtones ont été invoqués contre des projets miniers qui menaçaient leurs territoires, leurs ressources et leur autonomie politique. || Indigenous peoples’ rights were invoked against mining projects that threatened their territories, resources, and political autonomy.
NOTE: Materially updates old_back’s droits des indigènes to the current institutional formula. Self-review retained peuples because the example concerns collective territorial and political rights.

