121 the Patron-client relations => les relations de clientèle | alts=les relations patron-client | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les relations de clientèle
REG: Specialist political-science term for an asymmetrical exchange of protection or resources for loyalty; the hyphenated alternative is also used in academic writing.
TRAP: Here patron is a political protector and client a dependent actor, not an employer and customer; relations entre patrons et clients would select the commercial sense.
EX: Les relations de clientèle ont permis à Moscou de conserver une influence décisive sur des régimes dépendants sans les administrer directement. || Patron-client relations allowed Moscow to retain decisive influence over dependent regimes without administering them directly.
NOTE: Old_back gives an accepted technical calque. Self-review selected relations de clientèle as the more idiomatic headword while retaining the established hyphenated form as an alternative.
122 the Amazon deforestation => la déforestation en Amazonie | alts=la déforestation de l’Amazonie | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La déforestation en Amazonie
REG: Neutral environmental-policy phrase; en Amazonie locates the process, while de l’Amazonie presents the region as the forest area being depleted.
TRAP: Amazonie names the region, whereas Amazone names the river; the geographical construction normally uses en Amazonie, not dans l’Amazonie.
EX: La déforestation en Amazonie s’est accélérée lorsque le régime militaire a ouvert de nouveaux territoires à l’élevage, aux mines et à la spéculation foncière. || Deforestation in the Amazon accelerated when the military regime opened new territories to cattle farming, mining, and land speculation.
NOTE: Old_back is possible, but self-review preferred en Amazonie because it unambiguously locates the process rather than suggesting the complete removal of the region’s forest.
123 the Integration => l’intégration | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’intégration
REG: Neutral-formal policy noun whose intended object should normally be made explicit, as in intégration régionale, économique, or nationale.
TRAP: English integration of X into Y is usually l’intégration de X à or dans Y; French learners should not reproduce the English sequence with en in every context.
EX: L’intégration régionale devait renforcer le poids diplomatique de l’Amérique latine, mais les rivalités nationales ont longtemps limité la portée des accords conclus. || Regional integration was intended to strengthen Latin America’s diplomatic weight, but national rivalries long limited the scope of the agreements reached.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. The exact-duplicate report assigns this source prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB; self-review supplied a specific regional context to make the generic noun teachable.
124 the Literacy => la maîtrise de la lecture et de l’écriture | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La maîtrise de la lecture et de l’écriture
REG: Neutral educational and development-policy wording for the ability itself; taux d’alphabétisation is conventional when reporting a population statistic.
TRAP: Alphabétisation primarily names the teaching process or its statistical outcome, while littératie is a specialist term with uneven currency in France French.
EX: La maîtrise de la lecture et de l’écriture progressait, mais les écarts entre campagnes et villes continuaient de restreindre l’accès aux emplois qualifiés. || Literacy was improving, but disparities between rural and urban areas continued to restrict access to skilled jobs.
NOTE: Materially diverges from old_back, which gives the process alphabétisation rather than the underlying ability. Self-review retained the conventional statistical use only in the register guidance.
130 the Evangelical rise => l’essor de l’évangélisme | alts=la progression de l’évangélisme; la montée en puissance des évangéliques | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’essor de l’évangélisme
REG: Neutral journalistic and social-science phrase; the second alternative shifts the focus from the religious current to its adherents as political actors.
TRAP: Les évangéliques are evangelical believers, whereas les évangélistes are evangelists or preachers; the English adjective cannot be transferred mechanically.
EX: L’essor de l’évangélisme a reconfiguré le débat public brésilien, donnant à de nouvelles Églises un poids croissant dans les campagnes électorales. || The rise of evangelical Christianity reshaped Brazilian public debate, giving new churches growing weight in election campaigns.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate but less idiomatic as a headword. Self-review chose essor and kept montée en puissance only with a human plural subject.
131 the Illiteracy => l’analphabétisme | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’analphabétisme
REG: Neutral-formal education and development term for being unable to read or write; common in demographic measures such as taux d’analphabétisme.
TRAP: French distinguishes analphabétisme, never having learned to read and write, from illettrisme, seriously inadequate command after schooling.
EX: L’analphabétisme reculait dans les statistiques nationales, tandis que l’insuffisance des écoles rurales perpétuait de profondes inégalités entre les régions. || Illiteracy was declining in national statistics, while the inadequacy of rural schools perpetuated deep inequalities between regions.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review added the institutionally useful distinction from illettrisme rather than treating the two terms as free synonyms.
132 the Frontier expansion => l’avancée du front pionnier | alts=l’expansion du front pionnier | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’avancée du front pionnier
REG: Specialist human-geography phrase for the outward movement of settlement and exploitation into a previously less developed area.
TRAP: Expansion des frontières means enlargement of political borders; the Amazonian development process is the advance of a front pionnier.
EX: L’avancée du front pionnier en Amazonie a déplacé des communautés autochtones, fragmenté la forêt et renforcé l’emprise de grands propriétaires sur les terres. || Frontier expansion in the Amazon displaced Indigenous communities, fragmented the forest, and strengthened large landowners’ control over the land.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which describes a change in state borders. Self-review selected the established geographical concept front pionnier and an idiomatic collocation with avancée.
133 the Detente => la détente | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La détente
REG: Standard diplomatic-history term for the easing of East–West tensions, especially from the 1960s to the late 1970s.
TRAP: In this domain détente is a historical diplomatic phase, not merely relaxation or reduced personal stress; English borrowed the French noun without its article.
EX: La détente a multiplié les canaux diplomatiques entre les deux blocs sans supprimer ni la compétition stratégique ni les conflits menés par alliés interposés. || Détente multiplied diplomatic channels between the two blocs without eliminating either strategic competition or conflicts waged through allied intermediaries.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review made the specialist historical sense explicit and avoided presenting détente as the end of Cold War competition.
134 the Espionage => l’espionnage | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=L’espionnage
REG: Neutral-formal security and intelligence term for the covert acquisition of protected information on behalf of a state or organization.
TRAP: Espionnage names the activity, renseignement the information or intelligence field, and espion the person; these nouns are related but not interchangeable.
EX: L’espionnage industriel complétait les opérations militaires, car les technologies civiles pouvaient modifier rapidement l’équilibre stratégique entre les puissances rivales. || Industrial espionage supplemented military operations because civilian technologies could rapidly alter the strategic balance between rival powers.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. The exact-duplicate report assigns this prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB; self-review distinguished the activity from renseignement.
135 the Proxy war => la guerre par procuration | alts=le conflit par procuration | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La guerre par procuration
REG: Standard international-relations term for a conflict in which outside powers support local belligerents rather than confronting one another directly.
TRAP: The fixed construction is par procuration; old_back’s guerre des procurations incorrectly treats procuration as the object or participants of the war.
EX: La guerre par procuration permettait aux superpuissances d’armer leurs alliés régionaux tout en limitant le risque d’un affrontement militaire direct entre elles. || Proxy war allowed the superpowers to arm their regional allies while limiting the risk of a direct military confrontation between them.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back’s nonstandard plural construction. Self-review retained conflit par procuration as a true alternative when the armed confrontation is not formally called a war.
136 the Iron Curtain => le rideau de fer | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le rideau de fer
REG: Established historical metaphor for the political, military, and ideological division between Soviet-dominated Eastern Europe and the West.
TRAP: French uses rideau, not the Italian cortina or a literal iron screen; the historical expression is normally lower-case in running text.
EX: Le rideau de fer a séparé des sociétés voisines, transformant les frontières européennes en dispositifs militaires, policiers et idéologiques presque infranchissables. || The Iron Curtain separated neighboring societies, turning European borders into military, police, and ideological systems that were almost impossible to cross.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review normalized the common-noun capitalization used in standard French prose.
137 the Nuclear arms race => la course aux armements nucléaires | alts=la course aux armes nucléaires | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La course aux armements nucléaires
REG: Standard diplomatic and strategic-studies term; armements is the more institutional choice, while armes focuses more concretely on weapons.
TRAP: French says course aux armements, with à plus the plural article, rather than copying English word order as course nucléaire des armes.
EX: La course aux armements nucléaires a absorbé des ressources immenses et rendu chaque innovation défensive susceptible de provoquer une nouvelle escalade offensive. || The nuclear arms race absorbed immense resources and made each defensive innovation liable to provoke a new offensive escalation.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review kept armes nucléaires only as a slightly less institutional but contextually interchangeable alternative.
139 the Domino theory => la théorie des dominos | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La théorie des dominos
REG: Established Cold War historical term for the claim that one country’s communist takeover would trigger similar changes in neighboring states.
TRAP: The established French name uses the plural des dominos, not the literal singular théorie du domino suggested by English’s attributive noun.
EX: La théorie des dominos a servi à présenter chaque victoire communiste locale comme le prélude mécanique à l’effondrement politique des États voisins. || The domino theory served to present each local communist victory as the mechanical prelude to the political collapse of neighboring states.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review framed the theory as a historical claim rather than endorsing its causal assumptions.
140 the Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD) => la destruction mutuelle assurée (MAD) | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La destruction mutuelle assurée
REG: Specialist nuclear-strategy term; French strategic writing commonly retains the memorable English acronym MAD after translating the phrase.
TRAP: Do not mechanically replace MAD with DMA: French institutional and strategic sources commonly retain the English acronym even beside the translated term.
EX: La destruction mutuelle assurée reposait sur la certitude qu’une première frappe déclencherait des représailles capables d’anéantir aussi l’agresseur. || Mutually assured destruction rested on the certainty that a first strike would trigger retaliation capable of annihilating the aggressor as well.
NOTE: Old_back translates the phrase accurately. Self-review retained MAD because French diplomatic sources use that English acronym and kept it outside the clozed occurrence because the example does not spell it out.
142 the Deterrence => la dissuasion | alts=la dissuasion stratégique | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La dissuasion
REG: Standard defense and strategic-studies term; the qualified alternative is useful when the context does not already establish a military doctrine.
TRAP: English deterrence is dissuasion in strategic French, not the nonexistent calque déterrence; découragement is a general effect rather than the doctrine.
EX: La dissuasion exigeait que l’adversaire croie à la capacité de riposte, mais aussi à la volonté politique d’employer effectivement cette capacité. || Deterrence required the adversary to believe in both the capacity to retaliate and the political will actually to use that capacity.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review avoided dissuasion nucléaire as an unqualified alternative because deterrence may also be conventional.
143 the Non-aligned movement => le Mouvement des pays non alignés | alts=le Mouvement des non-alignés | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le Mouvement des pays non alignés
REG: Official name of the intergovernmental grouping in UN usage; the shorter alternative is an established historical and journalistic form.
TRAP: In the official form, pays is explicit and non alignés agrees with it; in the shorter form, les non-alignés is a substantivized plural and conventionally hyphenated.
EX: Le Mouvement des pays non alignés cherchait à préserver une autonomie diplomatique, même si ses membres divergeaient profondément sur leurs alliances et leurs modèles économiques. || The Non-Aligned Movement sought to preserve diplomatic autonomy, even though its members differed profoundly over their alliances and economic models.
NOTE: Self-review replaced old_back’s common shorter form with the official UN name Mouvement des pays non alignés and retained Mouvement des non-alignés as an established alternative.
144 the Satellite state => l’État satellite | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Un État satellite
REG: Neutral-formal but politically loaded term for a nominally sovereign state under another power’s decisive influence.
TRAP: Satellite follows the noun in French; it is not a literal celestial object here, and the plural is États satellites.
EX: Un État satellite conservait ses institutions nationales, mais ses choix stratégiques restaient étroitement subordonnés aux intérêts de la puissance tutélaire. || A satellite state retained its national institutions, but its strategic choices remained closely subordinated to the interests of the controlling power.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review used an indefinite article in the example because it defines a type rather than naming one particular state.
145 the Sphere of influence => la sphère d’influence | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=sphère d’influence
REG: Standard international-relations term; zone d’influence is close but tends to evoke a more geographically bounded area.
TRAP: Sphère d’influence can include political, economic, or cultural leverage and is therefore broader than direct territorial control or formal sovereignty.
EX: Chaque superpuissance considérait sa sphère d’influence comme un espace réservé, où toute intervention adverse risquait d’être interprétée comme une provocation. || Each superpower regarded its sphere of influence as a reserved domain where any opposing intervention risked being interpreted as a provocation.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review removed zone d’influence from alts because its more bounded geographical scope makes it an explanatory near-synonym rather than a fully interchangeable answer.
146 the Defectors => les transfuges | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les transfuges
REG: Neutral-formal political and intelligence term for people who abandon one state, camp, or organization and join its adversary.
TRAP: A transfuge changes allegiance; a réfugié flees danger and a déserteur abandons military service, so neither is automatically a defector.
EX: Les transfuges fournissaient parfois des renseignements précieux, mais les services occidentaux redoutaient toujours qu’ils aient été envoyés pour diffuser de fausses informations. || Defectors sometimes provided valuable intelligence, but Western services always feared that they had been sent to spread false information.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review excluded dissidents and refugees from the alternatives because opposition or flight alone does not entail a change of allegiance.
147 the Intelligence => le renseignement | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le renseignement
REG: Standard security term for intelligence as collected and analyzed information or as a field of state activity; often singular in French.
TRAP: Intelligence is a false friend here: l’intelligence means intellectual ability or understanding, while an intelligence agency is un service de renseignement.
EX: Le renseignement reposait autant sur l’analyse patiente de sources ouvertes que sur les informations secrètes recueillies par des agents opérant clandestinement. || Intelligence relied as much on the patient analysis of open sources as on secret information collected by agents operating clandestinely.
NOTE: Old_back correctly avoids the English false friend. Self-review kept the singular headword and explained the institutional construction separately.
148 the Underground => la résistance clandestine | alts=l’opposition clandestine | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La résistance clandestine
REG: Historical and political term for organized covert opposition; the alternative is broader and need not imply an armed or formally coordinated resistance.
TRAP: Le souterrain denotes an underground passage or something below ground; it does not name a clandestine political movement as English the underground can.
EX: La résistance clandestine diffusait des tracts, organisait des filières de fuite et maintenait un espace politique hors du contrôle du régime. || The underground distributed leaflets, organized escape networks, and maintained a political space beyond the regime’s control.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back’s physical sense. Self-review selected résistance clandestine for the organized political referent and changed the alternative to the grammatically interchangeable opposition clandestine.
149 the Disinformation => la désinformation | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La désinformation
REG: Neutral-formal media, security, and political term for deliberately deceptive information or the operation that disseminates it.
TRAP: Désinformation implies an intention to deceive; it is narrower than information erronée, which may be false through mistake rather than manipulation.
EX: La désinformation soviétique exploitait des récits plausibles, relayés par des intermédiaires crédibles afin de dissimuler leur origine et d’influencer les opinions occidentales. || Soviet disinformation exploited plausible narratives relayed by credible intermediaries in order to conceal their origin and influence Western public opinion.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. The exact-duplicate report assigns this prompt to COLD_WAR_VOCAB rather than FANCY_VOCAB; self-review made intentional deception central to the example.
150 the Buffer zone => la zone tampon | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=zone tampon
REG: Standard geography and security term for an intervening area intended to separate rival forces, territories, or systems.
TRAP: French forms the compound directly as zone tampon, without de; État tampon is a related term but names a sovereign polity rather than an area.
EX: Une zone tampon devait éloigner les forces rivales de la frontière, mais elle pouvait aussi limiter durablement la souveraineté des États concernés. || A buffer zone was intended to keep rival forces away from the border, but it could also durably limit the sovereignty of the states concerned.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review excluded État tampon as an alternative because it changes the type of political entity denoted.
151 the Arms control => la maîtrise des armements | alts=le contrôle des armements | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La maîtrise des armements
REG: Specialist diplomatic and defense term; maîtrise is the established institutional rendering, while contrôle is also used and can foreground constraints or verification.
TRAP: Arms means armements in this policy domain, not merely armes; old_back’s contrôle des armes sounds like oversight of individual weapons rather than strategic arms control.
EX: La maîtrise des armements ne visait pas nécessairement à supprimer les arsenaux, mais à plafonner certaines capacités et à rendre leur évolution prévisible. || Arms control did not necessarily aim to eliminate arsenals, but to cap certain capabilities and make their development predictable.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back by using the established strategic-policy term. Self-review retained contrôle des armements because French official sources also use it, with a slight emphasis difference.
152 the Space race => la course à l’espace | alts=la course spatiale | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La course à l’espace
REG: Standard historical and journalistic term for the technological competition in space exploration, especially between the United States and Soviet Union.
TRAP: French says course à l’espace, not course de l’espace; course spatiale is acceptable but can also describe a race event in speculative contexts.
EX: La course à l’espace a transformé les exploits scientifiques en démonstrations de puissance, tout en accélérant le développement des satellites civils et militaires. || The space race turned scientific achievements into demonstrations of power while accelerating the development of civilian and military satellites.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained course spatiale as an established alternative but flagged its greater contextual ambiguity.
154 the Missile crisis => la crise des missiles de Cuba | alts=la crise de Cuba | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La crise des missiles de Cuba
REG: Established historical name for the October 1962 confrontation; the shorter alternative is common when the Cold War context is already clear.
TRAP: The bare crise des missiles can describe other missile confrontations; adding de Cuba identifies the event conventionally called the Missile Crisis in English.
EX: La crise des missiles de Cuba a montré qu’une communication lente ou ambiguë pouvait rapprocher les superpuissances d’un affrontement nucléaire involontaire. || The Cuban Missile Crisis showed that slow or ambiguous communication could bring the superpowers closer to an unintended nuclear confrontation.
NOTE: Old_back gives a literal but underspecified phrase. Self-review restored the conventional event name by adding de Cuba, as selected by the definite Cold War prompt.
155 the Nuclear proliferation => la prolifération nucléaire | alts=la prolifération des armes nucléaires | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La prolifération nucléaire
REG: Standard international-security term; the longer alternative explicitly states that weapons, capabilities, or programs are spreading.
TRAP: In this collocation nucléaire characterizes weapons proliferation, not the spread of civilian nuclear energy; context may require the fuller construction to remove ambiguity.
EX: La prolifération nucléaire inquiétait les deux blocs, qui craignaient que de nouveaux États dotés de l’arme atomique ne rendent la dissuasion moins prévisible. || Nuclear proliferation worried both blocs, which feared that new nuclear-armed states would make deterrence less predictable.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained the more explicit alternative because the compact adjective can be ambiguous outside an international-security context.
157 Glasnost (openness) => la glasnost | alts=la politique de transparence | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La glasnost
REG: Established feminine historical borrowing for Gorbachev’s policy of greater public openness; normally lower-case in French running text.
TRAP: In this Soviet policy context openness is transparence, not literal ouverture; glasnost is a common noun in French and does not require an initial capital.
EX: La glasnost a permis aux médias soviétiques d’aborder des crimes longtemps occultés, fragilisant ainsi les récits officiels qui légitimaient le régime. || Glasnost allowed the Soviet media to address long-concealed crimes, thereby weakening the official narratives that legitimized the regime.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back’s capitalization and avoids presenting ouverture as the idiomatic policy gloss. Self-review follows standard dictionary treatment of glasnost as a feminine noun.
158 Perestroika (restructuring) => la perestroïka | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La perestroïka
REG: Established feminine historical borrowing for Gorbachev’s program of restructuring the Soviet political and economic system.
TRAP: Standard France French spelling uses a diaeresis in perestroïka; restructuration explains the Russian word but does not by itself name the specific Soviet program.
EX: La perestroïka a tenté de réformer l’économie soviétique sans démanteler immédiatement les structures politiques qui entravaient l’initiative et protégeaient les bureaucraties établies. || Perestroika attempted to reform the Soviet economy without immediately dismantling the political structures that hindered initiative and protected established bureaucracies.
NOTE: Old_back has the correct French spelling but treats restructuration as if it were part of the headword. Self-review kept the gloss in the register explanation instead.
159 the Hotline => le téléphone rouge | alts=la ligne directe Washington-Moscou | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le téléphone rouge
REG: Conventional French historical label for the direct crisis-communication link between Washington and Moscow; the alternative is technically more literal.
TRAP: Despite its French name, the original hotline was not a red voice telephone; ligne directe avoids turning the popular image into a technical description.
EX: Le téléphone rouge a réduit le risque de malentendu en permettant aux dirigeants américains et soviétiques d’échanger rapidement pendant les crises internationales. || The hotline reduced the risk of misunderstanding by allowing American and Soviet leaders to communicate quickly during international crises.
NOTE: Materially diverges from old_back only in register: ligne directe is technically accurate, while téléphone rouge is the established French historical label. Self-review explicitly warns against reading that label literally.

