### fr b05
164 the Nuclear testing => les essais nucléaires | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les essais nucléaires
REG: Standard defense and diplomatic-history term, normally plural when referring to a state’s weapons-testing program or the practice generally.
TRAP: In this Cold War context, essai means a weapons test; test nucléaire can also describe a technical test involving nuclear technology and is less historically specific.
EX: Les essais nucléaires atmosphériques ont exposé des populations éloignées aux retombées radioactives, alors même que les gouvernements minimisaient les risques sanitaires. || Atmospheric nuclear tests exposed remote populations to radioactive fallout even as governments played down the health risks.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained the conventional plural, made the weapons-testing sense explicit, and replaced a needlessly literary past anterior with an idiomatic imperfect.
167 the Conventional forces => les forces conventionnelles | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les forces conventionnelles
REG: Standard strategic-studies term for military forces and weapons that are not nuclear, biological, or chemical.
TRAP: Here conventionnelles means non-nuclear and related non-WMD forces, not forces that are merely usual, traditional, or compliant with a convention.
EX: Les forces conventionnelles massées en Europe centrale devaient pouvoir ralentir une offensive sans déclencher immédiatement un échange nucléaire entre les superpuissances. || The conventional forces massed in Central Europe were expected to slow an offensive without immediately triggering a nuclear exchange between the superpowers.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review defined the term by its strategic contrast and avoided implying that conventional necessarily means technologically obsolete.
169 the De-escalation => la désescalade | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=La désescalade
REG: Neutral-formal diplomatic and security term for a deliberate reduction in the intensity or danger of a confrontation.
TRAP: The established noun is désescalade; mechanically importing English de-escalation as dé-escalation or désescalation is unnecessary in standard France French.
EX: La désescalade exigeait le retrait progressif des missiles, un calendrier vérifiable et des canaux capables de prévenir tout malentendu susceptible de relancer la confrontation. || De-escalation required the gradual withdrawal of the missiles, a verifiable timetable, and channels capable of preventing any misunderstanding that could reignite the confrontation.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review replaced a vague reference to hostile interpretation with a concrete escalation risk and excluded réduction des tensions because it need not entail reciprocal action.
170 the Nuclear fallout => les retombées radioactives | alts=les retombées nucléaires | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Les retombées radioactives
REG: Standard scientific, historical, and policy term for radioactive material descending after a nuclear explosion; the alternative is common but less physically explicit.
TRAP: Retombées can mean beneficial or harmful consequences figuratively, but retombées radioactives denotes the physical contamination and is normally plural.
EX: Les retombées radioactives ont contaminé des pâturages situés à des milliers de kilomètres du site, révélant le caractère transfrontalier des essais atmosphériques. || Radioactive fallout contaminated pastures thousands of kilometers from the site, revealing the transboundary nature of atmospheric testing.
NOTE: Self-review replaced old_back’s less precise retombées nucléaires with the standard physical term and retained the legacy form only as a contextually valid alternative.
171 the Nuclear umbrella => le parapluie nucléaire | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=Le parapluie nucléaire
REG: Established strategic and journalistic metaphor for extended deterrence supplied by a nuclear power to its allies.
TRAP: A parapluie nucléaire is a deterrent guarantee, not a physical shield that intercepts missiles; défense antimissile names the latter concept.
EX: Le parapluie nucléaire américain rassurait les alliés européens, mais alimentait aussi le débat sur leur dépendance stratégique envers Washington. || The American nuclear umbrella reassured European allies, but it also fueled debate about their strategic dependence on Washington.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review defined the metaphor through extended deterrence and explicitly separated it from missile defense.
173 Cold peace => la paix froide | alts=— | lexical-noun | CLOZE=une paix froide
REG: Neutral-formal analytical metaphor for a relationship without open war but still marked by hostility, mistrust, and little cooperation.
TRAP: Paix froide describes a condition and is not another name for la guerre froide, the historically specific geopolitical confrontation.
EX: Après l’accord, une paix froide s’est installée entre les deux États, sans coopération durable ni véritable normalisation de leurs relations diplomatiques. || After the agreement, a cold peace settled between the two states, without lasting cooperation or any genuine normalization of their diplomatic relations.
NOTE: Old_back gives the right phrase but capitalizes it unnecessarily. Self-review used an indefinite article in the example because it describes a newly established condition.
174 to confront => affronter | alts=faire face à | lexical-verb | CLOZE=affronter
REG: Neutral and direct for facing an adversary, danger, or difficult reality; faire face à is slightly less combative and equally standard.
TRAP: Confronter is not a blanket equivalent of English confront: French normally confronts two accounts or confronte quelqu’un à a fact, but affronte a danger.
EX: Les dirigeants américains ont dû affronter un risque croissant de guerre nucléaire tout en maintenant un canal discret avec Moscou. || American leaders had to confront a growing risk of nuclear war while maintaining a discreet channel with Moscow.
NOTE: The source is polysemous; this card selects confronting a danger or difficult reality. Self-review replaced the less idiomatic possibilité with risque and retained only an alternative that fits unchanged.
175 to oppose => s’opposer à | alts=s’élever contre | lexical-verb | CLOZE=se sont opposés à
REG: Neutral-formal and standard in policy prose; s’élever contre is more rhetorical and emphasizes publicly voicing opposition.
TRAP: Unlike transitive English oppose, French requires the pronominal verb and à: s’opposer à une mesure, not opposer une mesure in this sense.
EX: Plusieurs diplomates se sont opposés à l’installation de nouveaux missiles, estimant qu’elle réduirait dangereusement le temps disponible pour toute décision. || Several diplomats opposed the deployment of new missiles, judging that it would dangerously reduce the time available for any decision.
NOTE: Old_back gives the right verb but omits its governed preposition. Self-review verified that the alternative can replace the main expression in this public-policy context after normal inflection.
176 to align => s’aligner sur | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=s’est aligné sur
REG: Neutral-formal geopolitical and policy expression, often implying that an actor adopts another power’s position or strategic line.
TRAP: When one actor adopts another’s position or model, French conventionally uses sur; avec can express coordination, so it should not replace sur mechanically here.
EX: Après le coup d’État, le nouveau régime s’est aligné sur la politique de Moscou afin d’obtenir des armes, des crédits et une protection diplomatique. || After the coup, the new regime aligned itself with Moscow’s policy in order to obtain weapons, credit, and diplomatic protection.
NOTE: Old_back gives the right pronominal verb but omits sur. Dictionary review confirmed the policy-conformity frame; self-review added politique to make that selected sense unmistakable.
177 to infiltrate => infiltrer | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=ont infiltré
REG: Neutral-formal intelligence and policing verb when a service covertly penetrates an organization or network.
TRAP: A service can infiltrer un mouvement directly, whereas an agent who enters it covertly commonly s’infiltre dans le mouvement; the constructions shift the viewpoint.
EX: Les services de renseignement ont infiltré le mouvement étudiant afin d’identifier ses réseaux de financement et d’anticiper ses campagnes contre le régime. || The intelligence services infiltrated the student movement to identify its funding networks and anticipate its campaigns against the regime.
NOTE: Old_back supplies only the bare transitive lemma. Self-review selected the institutional transitive viewpoint, preferred services de renseignement, and kept the agent-centered reflexive construction as a contrast.
183 to resist => résister à | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=ont résisté à
REG: Neutral and widely used for withstanding pressure, coercion, occupation, or an adversary; the complement is introduced by à.
TRAP: French résister is indirect: résister à la pression. English resist and Spanish resistir take direct objects, a strong cross-language interference risk.
EX: Malgré la censure, des rédactions clandestines ont résisté à la pression du parti et continué de documenter les arrestations politiques. || Despite censorship, underground newsrooms resisted pressure from the party and continued to document political arrests.
NOTE: Old_back omits the governed preposition. Self-review kept the card because the indirect construction remains a live production trap even though the verb itself is common.
185 to embargo => imposer un embargo à | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=ont imposé un embargo | au
REG: Neutral-formal diplomatic and economic-policy construction for subjecting a state, regime, or trade category to an embargo.
TRAP: English freely verbalizes embargo; standard formal French normally uses imposer un embargo à or mettre sous embargo rather than a bare verb.
EX: Après l’invasion, plusieurs États ont imposé un embargo au régime, mais des intermédiaires commerciaux ont continué d’acheminer des composants stratégiques. || After the invasion, several states imposed an embargo on the regime, but commercial intermediaries continued to transport strategic components.
NOTE: Old_back’s mettre sous embargo is valid, but self-review chose the more institutional support-verb frame, removed a distracting compound-noun spelling issue, and clozed both fixed arms.
188 to protest => manifester contre | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=ont manifesté contre
REG: Neutral political and journalistic expression for taking part in a public demonstration against a measure, institution, or government.
TRAP: English protest covers both demonstrating and objecting verbally; French manifester guarantees public collective action, while protester may denote only an expressed objection.
EX: Dans plusieurs capitales, des étudiants ont manifesté contre la censure malgré les arrestations préventives et la présence massive de policiers en uniforme. || In several capitals, students protested against censorship despite preventive arrests and the massive presence of uniformed police officers.
NOTE: The English prompt is ambiguous. Guided by the political topic, self-review selected public demonstration; old_back’s protester remains valid for the narrower act of voicing an objection.
192 to dissent => exprimer son désaccord avec | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=ont exprimé leur désaccord avec
REG: Neutral-formal expression for openly stating disagreement with a position, decision, doctrine, or institutional consensus.
TRAP: Standard France French normally paraphrases the verb; dissidence is a noun for more sustained or organized opposition and cannot translate the verbal prompt by itself.
EX: Trois membres du comité ont exprimé leur désaccord avec la doctrine officielle, au risque de compromettre leur carrière diplomatique. || Three committee members dissented from the official doctrine, at the risk of jeopardizing their diplomatic careers.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which supplies a noun instead of a production-ready verbal expression. Self-review chose an overt speech-act sense rather than the weaker être en désaccord.
193 to exile => exiler | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=a exilé
REG: Neutral-formal transitive verb when an authority forces someone to live outside their country or customary place of residence.
TRAP: Exiler is transitive and assigns agency to the authority; s’exiler makes the displaced person the subject and may describe a constrained or voluntary departure.
EX: Après le procès, le régime a exilé plusieurs intellectuels influents, espérant ainsi priver l’opposition de relais crédibles auprès des médias étrangers. || After the trial, the regime exiled several influential intellectuals, hoping thereby to deprive the opposition of credible contacts in foreign media.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate for the transitive reading. Self-review excluded s’exiler from alts because its change of subject and agency prevents direct interchangeability in the example.
196 to deter => dissuader quelqu’un de | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=dissuader | de
REG: Neutral-formal construction for deterring an actor from an action; in strategy, the related doctrine is la dissuasion.
TRAP: French requires both the person deterred and de before an infinitive: dissuader les dirigeants de reprendre, not a direct calque without de.
EX: La perspective de sanctions coordonnées devait dissuader les dirigeants de reprendre les essais, sans toutefois leur offrir une voie de sortie négociée. || The prospect of coordinated sanctions was meant to deter the leaders from resuming the tests, without offering them a negotiated way out.
NOTE: Old_back gives the correct verb but omits its two-part government. Self-review clozed both fixed arms and avoided empêcher, which would imply successful prevention rather than deterrence.
197 to escalate => intensifier | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=a intensifié
REG: Neutral-formal transitive verb for increasing the intensity of a conflict, crisis, or campaign; the intransitive counterpart is s’intensifier.
TRAP: Escalader normally means to climb or scale a physical obstacle; escalader un conflit is an avoidable English calque in careful France French.
EX: La livraison de missiles supplémentaires a intensifié le conflit, réduisant les possibilités de négociation et augmentant le risque d’une intervention directe des superpuissances. || The delivery of additional missiles escalated the conflict, reducing the possibilities for negotiation and increasing the risk of direct superpower intervention.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back’s escalader. Dictionary review confirmed that the figurative strategic sense belongs to escalade, not the standard verb; self-review selected the transitive conflict sense.
198 to proliferate => proliférer | alts=se multiplier | lexical-verb | CLOZE=ont proliféré
REG: Neutral-formal to specialist verb for rapid, often undesirable multiplication or spread; common in arms-control and security analysis.
TRAP: French proliférer is intransitive: programs or weapons prolifèrent; to express an external cause, use favoriser la prolifération rather than proliférer quelque chose.
EX: Après la rupture du traité, les programmes clandestins ont proliféré, compliquant les efforts internationaux de vérification et affaiblissant le régime de non-prolifération. || After the treaty collapsed, clandestine programs proliferated, complicating international verification efforts and weakening the non-proliferation regime.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review made the intransitive restriction explicit and retained se multiplier because it can replace the main verb in this example after normal inflection.
200 to transition => passer de … à … | alts=— | lexical-expression | CLOZE=sont passés d’ | à
REG: Neutral and idiomatic for political, economic, or institutional change when both the starting point and destination are stated.
TRAP: Do not mechanically render a generic economic or policy transition as transitionner; careful standard prose prefers passer de … à … or a construction with the noun transition.
EX: Après 1989, plusieurs États sont passés d’une économie planifiée à une économie de marché sans construire immédiatement des institutions de régulation solides. || After 1989, several states transitioned from a planned economy to a market economy without immediately building robust regulatory institutions.
NOTE: Materially corrects old_back, which supplies only the noun transition. Self-review selected a two-endpoint verbal frame, replaced the vague modèle de marché, and clozed each fixed arm separately.
### fr b06
202 to subvert => subvertir | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=subvertir
REG: Formal political and institutional verb for overturning an established order or corrupting an organization’s operation from within.
TRAP: Subvertir suggests a fundamental alteration of an established order; saper in item 222 instead emphasizes gradual weakening, while renverser denotes outright overthrow.
EX: Des réseaux clandestins cherchaient à subvertir l’ordre constitutionnel en infiltrant les syndicats, les médias et les administrations encore fragilisées par la guerre. || Clandestine networks sought to subvert the constitutional order by infiltrating trade unions, media outlets, and government departments still weakened by the war.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained this formal verb because its direct alteration of an established order contrasts materially with the gradual erosion expressed by saper in item 222.
206 to deforest => déboiser | alts=déforester | lexical-verb | CLOZE=déboisé
REG: Neutral environmental and land-use verb for removing forest cover from an area; déforester is a more technical alternative, especially for large-scale forest loss.
TRAP: Défricher adds the idea of clearing land for cultivation or development and is therefore not interchangeable with déboiser in every context.
EX: Le régime a déboisé de vastes zones frontalières pour ouvrir des routes militaires, accélérant l’érosion et le déplacement de communautés rurales. || The regime deforested vast border areas to open military roads, accelerating erosion and the displacement of rural communities.
NOTE: Old_back supplies the idiomatic neutral verb. Self-review retained déforester only as a contextually interchangeable technical alternative and separated both terms from the purpose-specific défricher.
208 to broadcast => diffuser | alts=émettre | lexical-verb | CLOZE=diffusait
REG: Neutral media verb for broadcasting content to an audience; émettre is equally natural here but foregrounds the station’s transmission activity.
TRAP: Retransmettre normally means to relay or rebroadcast a signal, program, or event and should not replace the generic English broadcast automatically.
EX: Pendant la crise, la station clandestine diffusait chaque soir des bulletins codés afin de coordonner l’opposition et de contourner la censure officielle. || During the crisis, the clandestine station broadcast coded bulletins every evening to coordinate the opposition and circumvent official censorship.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review kept émettre because it fits the same radio-station example and excluded retransmettre, which would imply relaying an event or another source’s signal.
209 to innovate => innover | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=innover
REG: Neutral-formal verb, commonly used absolutely or with dans or en matière de to specify the field in which innovation occurs.
TRAP: In careful French, innover is normally intransitive: one innove dans un domaine rather than mechanically calquing English innovate a technology with a direct object.
EX: Pour réduire sa dépendance technologique, le programme spatial devait innover dans les télécommunications sans importer de composants soumis aux contrôles occidentaux. || To reduce its technological dependence, the space program had to innovate in telecommunications without importing components subject to Western controls.
NOTE: Old_back gives the correct lemma but not its construction. Self-review retained the item for its consequential intransitive pattern and avoided claiming that every contemporary transitive use is impossible.
210 to impose => imposer quelque chose à quelqu’un | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=imposé | au
REG: Neutral-formal and highly productive in political prose for sanctions, restrictions, conditions, obligations, and financial burdens.
TRAP: English impose sanctions on a country maps to imposer des sanctions à un pays; sur cannot mechanically replace à before the affected recipient.
EX: Après l’essai nucléaire, plusieurs gouvernements ont imposé de nouvelles restrictions commerciales au régime, malgré les réserves de leurs industriels les plus exposés. || After the nuclear test, several governments imposed new trade restrictions on the regime, despite reservations from the industrial firms in their countries most exposed to the measures.
NOTE: Old_back supplies only the verb. Self-review retained the broader political frame beyond the earlier embargo expression and clozed the inflected verb and recipient preposition separately.
212 to retaliate => riposter à | alts=— | lexical-verb | CLOZE=a riposté aux
REG: Neutral-formal verb for responding forcefully to an attack, sanction, or hostile act; common in diplomatic and military reporting.
TRAP: French riposte à an act or measure; retaliation against an actor may instead require exercer des représailles contre, while se venger de sounds more personal.
EX: Le gouvernement a riposté aux sanctions économiques en expulsant deux diplomates, tout en évitant les mesures militaires susceptibles d’élargir la crise. || The government retaliated against the economic sanctions by expelling two diplomats while avoiding military measures liable to widen the crisis.
NOTE: Materially improves old_back ('pour riposter'), which includes a purpose marker rather than a production-ready headword. Self-review selected the trigger construction riposter à and kept actor-directed reprisals as a contrast.
215 to advocate => plaider pour | alts=préconiser; défendre | lexical-verb | CLOZE=ont plaidé pour
REG: Formal argumentative expression for publicly supporting a policy or course of action; préconiser recommends it, while défendre foregrounds active support.
TRAP: Plaider requires pour in this sense, whereas préconiser and défendre take a direct object; carrying pour over to either alternative produces the wrong construction.
EX: Plusieurs diplomates ont plaidé pour une levée progressive des sanctions, conditionnée à des inspections indépendantes et au démantèlement vérifiable des installations secrètes. || Several diplomats advocated a gradual lifting of sanctions, conditional on independent inspections and the verifiable dismantling of secret facilities.
NOTE: Materially refines old_back ('préconiser'): that verb fits the policy example, but plaider pour better teaches the advocacy frame. Self-review verified both direct-object alternatives in the same sentence after normal inflection.
217 to strategize => élaborer une stratégie | alts=mettre au point une stratégie | lexical-expression | CLOZE=ont élaboré une stratégie
REG: Neutral-formal support-verb expression for deliberately formulating a plan; the alternative emphasizes refining it until it is operational.
TRAP: English strategize normally becomes a support-verb expression in careful French; agir stratégiquement means to act strategically, not to formulate a strategy.
EX: Avant le sommet, les conseillers ont élaboré une stratégie commune pour exploiter les divisions adverses sans compromettre la reprise des négociations. || Before the summit, the advisers devised a common strategy to exploit divisions among their adversaries without jeopardizing the resumption of negotiations.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained the support-verb treatment, verified that the alternative fits before commune, and distinguished planning from merely acting in a strategic manner.
222 to undermine => saper | alts=miner; affaiblir | lexical-verb | CLOZE=sapé
REG: Saper and miner are formal figurative verbs suggesting progressive erosion; affaiblir is neutral and states the weakening without that metaphorical nuance.
TRAP: Saper does not always equal subvertir: undermine often weakens trust or capacity gradually, whereas subvertir in item 202 alters an established order more directly.
EX: Des campagnes de désinformation ont sapé la confiance dans les institutions électorales, transformant chaque défaite en preuve supposée d’une ingérence étrangère. || Disinformation campaigns undermined trust in electoral institutions, turning every defeat into supposed evidence of foreign interference.
NOTE: Old_back is accurate. Self-review removed subvertir as an alternative because item 202 tests a distinct sense and verified that miner and affaiblir both fit the example after inflection.

